export const STAGE0_UNIT_MD: Record<string, string> = {"u0":"# Stage 0 · Unit 0 — Welcome & Sounds (full lessons, v1.0)\n\n> **Spec task:** 1.3 · **Built on:** `curriculum/02-lesson-template.md` · **Map:** `stage0/00-stage0-unit-map.md`\n> **Arabic support:** ~70% (heavy). Explanations and instructions are in Egyptian Arabic; the learner\n> **outputs in English**. Arabic here is written in-line so the lesson is usable as-is.\n> **Unit goal:** understand classroom instructions, start producing the English sounds Arabic speakers\n> find hardest, and say basic survival phrases — building confidence from minute one.\n\n**Unit lessons:** L01 Welcome & how this works · L02 The alphabet & letter sounds · L03 Hard sounds (1):\n/p/–/b/, /v/–/f/ · L04 Survival phrases · L05 Numbers 0–20.\n\n**Assets to produce for this unit:** short welcome video (founder on camera), alphabet audio (American\nmodel), minimal-pair audio, survival-phrase audio, numbers audio, one Google Form quiz per lesson, a\ncommunity \"voice-note\" prompt per lesson.\n\n---\n\n## S0-U0-L01 — Welcome & How This Journey Works\n\n**Metadata:** Stage 0 / Pre-A1 · Unit 0 · ~25 min · recorded + live kickoff · prerequisites: none.\n\n**Objectives (can-do):**\n- The learner can explain (to themselves) how EEC works and what to do each week.\n- The learner can state their personal goal and their daily practice time.\n- The learner can say and record one sentence: *\"My name is ___ and I want to speak English.\"*\n\n**Target language:** minimal — the sentence frame *\"My name is ___. I want to speak English.\"*\n**Accent Lab focus:** none yet (awareness only — \"English has sounds we don't have; we'll train them\").\n**Arabic support:** ~90% (this is onboarding). **Mindset focus:** identity + consistency.\n\n**Materials:** founder welcome video; a one-page \"how it works\" graphic; goal-setting prompt; community channel link.\n\n**Lesson flow:**\n\n| # | Phase | Content | ~Time |\n|--|--|--|--|\n| 1 | Warm-up | Founder greets warmly (Arabic): *\"أهلاً بيك. إنت هتتكلم إنجليزي، خطوة بخطوة.\"* Set the tone: this is a system, not tricks. | 3 min |\n| 2 | Input | Welcome video: the promise (clear, confident English + American accent), the honesty (it takes consistency), and the founder's own story in brief. | 6 min |\n| 3 | Notice (how it works) | Explain the weekly rhythm (Arabic): recorded lessons + live speaking session + community + daily practice. Show the \"how it works\" graphic. | 5 min |\n| 4 | Mindset core | Teach identity (Arabic): *\"من النهاردة، إنت شخص بيتعلم الإنجليزي وبيتكلمه.\"* Explain: **consistency beats intensity** — 20 focused minutes daily > 3 hours once. | 4 min |\n| 5 | Guided output | Learner fills the frame aloud: *\"My name is ___. I want to speak English.\"* (model first, slow then natural). | 3 min |\n| 6 | Free output / commitment | Learner sets a goal + daily time and says it. Records the sentence + goal as a voice note. | 3 min |\n| 7 | Feedback | Self-check against model; keep this as a keepsake \"Day-1\" clip alongside the placement sample. | 1 min |\n| 8 | Assignment | Post your intro voice note + your goal in the community channel; say hello to 2 classmates. | — |\n\n**Differentiation:** Support — provide the full written sentence + audio. Stretch — add *\"I want to speak English for ___ (work / travel / study).\"*\n**Success check:** learner posts the voice note and a stated goal. **Homework → queue:** the frame *\"My name is…\"* returns in U1-L02.\n**Coach notes:** this lesson's job is emotional safety and commitment, not language. Celebrate every post. Reply personally to as many intros as possible — first impressions drive retention.\n\n---\n\n## S0-U0-L02 — The English Alphabet & Letter Sounds\n\n**Metadata:** Stage 0 / Pre-A1 · Unit 0 · ~30 min · recorded · prerequisites: L01.\n\n**Objectives (can-do):**\n- The learner can recognize and say the 26 letters of the English alphabet.\n- The learner can spell their own name aloud in English.\n- The learner can distinguish letter **name** vs letter **sound** (e.g., letter \"C\" = /siː/, sound often /k/).\n\n**Target language:** the alphabet A–Z; \"How do you spell…?\" / \"It's spelled ___.\"\n**Accent Lab focus:** letters Arabic speakers confuse — **B/P**, **F/V**, **G/J**, and the vowels **A E I O U**.\n**Arabic support:** ~70%. **Mindset focus:** small daily reps (5 letters a day).\n\n**Materials:** alphabet audio (American model, slow + natural); alphabet chart; \"spell your name\" activity; Google Form (match letter → sound).\n\n**Lesson flow:**\n\n| # | Phase | Content | ~Time |\n|--|--|--|--|\n| 1 | Warm-up / recall | Recall L01: say *\"My name is ___.\"* aloud. | 3 min |\n| 2 | Input | Listen to the full alphabet sung/said clearly; follow the chart. | 6 min |\n| 3 | Notice | Explain (Arabic) letter **name** vs **sound**; flag the tricky ones: B≠P, F≠V, G≠J, and the 5 vowels. | 6 min |\n| 4 | Accent Lab micro-drill | Repeat the confusable letters in pairs: B/P, F/V, G/J; record-and-compare. | 5 min |\n| 5 | Guided output | Spell simple words aloud (cat, book, name); then spell your **own name**. | 5 min |\n| 6 | Free output | \"How do you spell your name?\" — learner answers aloud and records it. | 3 min |\n| 7 | Feedback | Compare to model; note any letter to repeat tomorrow. | 1 min |\n| 8 | Assignment | Practice 5 letters/day; post a voice note spelling your name + your city. Quiz form. | 1 min |\n\n**Differentiation:** Support — group letters in small sets (A–G, H–N…). Stretch — spell a longer word (your full name / country).\n**Success check:** learner spells their name intelligibly. **Queue:** confusable letters feed L03 (B/P, F/V).\n**Coach notes:** Arabic has no /p/ or /v/ — expect \"B\" for \"P\" and \"F\" for \"V\". Don't over-correct yet; L03 targets these directly.\n\n---\n\n## S0-U0-L03 — Hard Sounds (1): /p/ vs /b/ and /v/ vs /f/\n\n**Metadata:** Stage 0 / Pre-A1 · Unit 0 · ~30 min · recorded + live reinforcement · prerequisites: L02.\n\n**Objectives (can-do):**\n- The learner can **hear** the difference between /p/–/b/ and /v/–/f/ in minimal pairs.\n- The learner can **produce** /p/ (with a puff of air) and /v/ (top teeth on lower lip).\n- The learner can say target words clearly: *pen, book, van, four*.\n\n**Target language:** words for the drills (pen/Ben, pig/big, pack/back; van/fan, vote/…, very, five, four).\n**Accent Lab focus:** /p/ vs /b/ (voiceless vs voiced + aspiration) and /v/ vs /f/ (voiced vs voiceless).\n**Arabic support:** ~70%. **Mindset focus:** \"your mouth is learning a new skill — reps build it.\"\n\n**Materials:** minimal-pair audio; a mirror tip (watch your lips); a paper-strip trick for /p/ (the puff moves the paper); record-and-compare tool.\n\n**Lesson flow:**\n\n| # | Phase | Content | ~Time |\n|--|--|--|--|\n| 1 | Warm-up / recall | Recall confusable letters from L02 (B/P, F/V). | 3 min |\n| 2 | Input | Listen to minimal pairs: **pen/Ben, pig/big, pack/back** and **van/fan, very/ferry, vote/…**. Just listen first. | 6 min |\n| 3 | Notice (articulation) | Explain (Arabic) **how the mouth makes each sound**: /p/ = lips together + **puff of air** (بينفخ هوا)، /b/ = same lips **without** the puff + voice. /v/ = **سنانك الفوق على شفتك التحت** + voice; /f/ = same place, no voice. | 6 min |\n| 4 | Accent Lab drill | Paper-strip test for /p/ (paper moves) vs /b/ (no move). Mirror for /v/–/f/. Repeat pairs; then record-and-compare. | 8 min |\n| 5 | Guided output | Say target words in slots: *\"a **pen**\", \"a **book**\", \"a **van**\", \"number **four/five**\".* | 4 min |\n| 6 | Free output | Say 4 words of your choice containing these sounds; record. | 2 min |\n| 7 | Feedback | Compare to model; pick your weakest sound → it returns in the review queue. | 1 min |\n| 8 | Assignment | 3-min daily mirror drill; post a voice note saying: *pen, book, van, four, five*. | — |\n\n**Differentiation:** Support — start with listening-only discrimination (point to the word you hear). Stretch — sentence: *\"I have a pen and a book.\"*\n**Success check:** intelligible /p/ and /v/ in the recorded words (rubric: Pronunciation). **Queue:** each learner's weakest of the two pairs is re-drilled in U1 warm-ups and Unit 10.\n**Coach notes:** This is the first taste of the Accent Lab — make it feel like a \"superpower unlock,\" not a correction of a flaw. Praise audible puffs and teeth-on-lip attempts.\n\n---\n\n## S0-U0-L04 — Survival Phrases: Staying in the Conversation\n\n**Metadata:** Stage 0 / Pre-A1 · Unit 0 · ~25 min · recorded + live · prerequisites: L01–L03.\n\n**Objectives (can-do):**\n- The learner can use phrases to keep a conversation alive when they don't understand.\n- The learner can ask for repetition and for how to say something.\n- The learner can respond politely (please / thank you / sorry).\n\n**Target language (survival set):**\n- *Hello / Hi* — أهلاً · *Please* — من فضلك · *Thank you* — شكراً · *Sorry / Excuse me* — آسف / لو سمحت\n- *Yes / No* — أيوه / لأ · *I don't understand* — أنا مش فاهم\n- *Can you repeat, please?* — ممكن تعيد تاني؟ · *Sorry?* (= repeat) — ممكن تاني؟\n- *How do you say ___ in English?* — إزاي تقول ___ بالإنجليزي؟ · *What does ___ mean?* — يعني إيه ___؟\n\n**Accent Lab focus:** clear /p/ in *please*; polite **rising intonation** on questions.\n**Arabic support:** ~70%. **Mindset focus:** \"you don't need to be perfect to communicate — stay in the game.\"\n\n**Materials:** survival-phrase audio + printable card; role-play prompts; record-and-compare.\n\n**Lesson flow:**\n\n| # | Phase | Content | ~Time |\n|--|--|--|--|\n| 1 | Warm-up / recall | Quick /p/–/v/ reps from L03 (say: pen, van). | 3 min |\n| 2 | Input | Listen to a short dialogue where someone uses survival phrases to cope; text + Arabic gloss. | 6 min |\n| 3 | Notice | Present the survival set with Arabic meanings; explain **when** to use each. | 5 min |\n| 4 | Accent Lab | Drill *\"please\"* (clear /p/) and rising intonation on *\"Can you repeat, please?\"* | 3 min |\n| 5 | Guided output | Substitution: react to prompts — teacher says a hard word → learner replies *\"Sorry? / Can you repeat, please?\"* | 5 min |\n| 6 | Free output | Mini role-play (live: in pairs): one speaks fast, the other survives using the phrases; record. | 3 min |\n| 7 | Feedback | Self-check; note the one phrase you'll use most this week. | — |\n| 8 | Assignment | Use one survival phrase in a real situation this week; post which one you used + how it felt. | — |\n\n**Differentiation:** Support — give the printable card to read from. Stretch — add *\"What does ___ mean?\"* in a real chat.\n**Success check:** learner produces ≥3 survival phrases intelligibly and appropriately. **Queue:** survival phrases recycle in every future warm-up (\"say two survival phrases\").\n**Coach notes:** This lesson massively lowers speaking anxiety — it gives learners permission to not understand everything. Reinforce heavily in the live session.\n\n---\n\n## S0-U0-L05 — Numbers 0–20 & Saying Them Clearly\n\n**Metadata:** Stage 0 / Pre-A1 · Unit 0 · ~30 min · recorded · prerequisites: L01–L04.\n\n**Objectives (can-do):**\n- The learner can say and recognize numbers 0–20.\n- The learner can give a phone number and their age.\n- The learner can distinguish tricky pairs (e.g., *thir**teen*** vs *thirty* comes in U2 — here: clear -teen).\n\n**Target language:** numbers 0–20; *\"My number is ___.\" \"I'm ___ years old.\"*\n**Accent Lab focus:** /θ/ in *three/thirteen* (preview of U2), clear final sounds; stress on numbers.\n**Arabic support:** ~70%. **Mindset focus:** celebrate the first-week win; preview Unit 1.\n\n**Materials:** numbers audio; number chart; \"say your phone number\" activity; Google Form (listen → write the number).\n\n**Lesson flow:**\n\n| # | Phase | Content | ~Time |\n|--|--|--|--|\n| 1 | Warm-up / recall | Say two survival phrases from L04; quick /p/–/v/ word. | 3 min |\n| 2 | Input | Listen to 0–20 said clearly; follow the chart; repeat in chunks (0–10, 11–20). | 7 min |\n| 3 | Notice | Highlight *three/thirteen* (/θ/ preview) and clear endings; explain (Arabic) the /θ/ tongue position lightly. | 5 min |\n| 4 | Accent Lab | Drill *three, thirteen* (tongue tip between teeth) + count 0–20 with a steady beat (rhythm). | 5 min |\n| 5 | Guided output | Say your **age** and a **phone number** (digit by digit). | 5 min |\n| 6 | Free output | Record: *\"I'm ___ years old. My number is ___.\"* | 3 min |\n| 7 | Feedback | Compare to model; note weak numbers. | 1 min |\n| 8 | Assignment | Count 0–20 daily; post a voice note with your age + a (fake if you prefer) phone number. Quiz form. | 1 min |\n\n**Differentiation:** Support — practice 0–10 first, then 11–20. Stretch — count 0–20 backwards.\n**Success check:** learner says 0–20 and their age intelligibly. **Queue:** numbers return in U2 (ages 20–100) and U6 (prices).\n**Coach notes:** End-of-unit moment — acknowledge that in one week they've produced English on camera, met classmates, and started fixing sounds. Preview Unit 1: \"Next week you'll fully introduce yourself in English.\"\n\n---\n\n## Unit 0 — end-of-unit check (formative)\n\n- **Quiz (Google Form):** letter→sound matches; hear-the-number; choose the right survival phrase.\n- **Speaking task (live or recorded):** say hello, spell your name, say your age, and use one survival\n  phrase (~1 min). Scored lightly on the rubric (Pronunciation + Fluency at Pre-A1/A1 threshold).\n- **Feeds:** the Cohort Tracker `week1_active` + early pronunciation notes.\n\n---\n\n*Next: Unit 1 — Introducing Yourself (L01–L05), authored in full on the same template.*\n","u1":"# Stage 0 · Unit 1 — Introducing Yourself (full lessons, v1.0)\n\n> **Spec task:** 1.3 · **Built on:** `curriculum/02-lesson-template.md` · **Map:** `stage0/00-stage0-unit-map.md`\n> **Arabic support:** ~70% (heavy), instructions/explanations in Egyptian Arabic; learner outputs in English.\n> **Unit goal:** by the end, the learner can fully introduce themselves in English — greet, give their\n> name, country, and job, and ask a person for the same — and has it recorded on video.\n> **Grammar spine:** verb **to be** (I'm / you're / he's / she's) · **Accent Lab:** /aɪ/ \"I'm\", word\n> stress · **Mindset:** the daily habit; consistency > intensity.\n\n**Unit lessons:** L01 Greetings & \"How are you?\" · L02 \"Hello, I'm…\" · L03 \"Where are you from?\" ·\nL04 \"What do you do?\" · L05 Unit speaking task + review.\n\n**Assets to produce:** dialogue videos (American models), sentence-frame slides, country/job flashcards,\nminimal audio for /aɪ/ and stress, a Google Form per lesson, community voice-note prompts.\n\n---\n\n## S0-U1-L01 — Greetings & \"How are you?\"\n\n**Metadata:** Stage 0 / Pre-A1→A1 · Unit 1 · ~30 min · recorded + live · prerequisites: Unit 0.\n\n**Objectives (can-do):**\n- The learner can greet at different times (hello / hi / good morning / afternoon / evening).\n- The learner can ask *\"How are you?\"* and answer (*\"I'm fine, thanks. And you?\"*).\n- The learner can say goodbye (bye / goodbye / see you).\n\n**Target language:** hello, hi, good morning/afternoon/evening; how are you?; fine/good/great/not bad;\nthanks; goodbye/bye/see you. **Grammar:** *to be* (I'm), fixed phrases.\n**Accent Lab focus:** clear /h/ in *hello/how*; friendly **rising→falling** intonation.\n**Arabic support:** ~70%. **Mindset focus:** the daily habit — pick your 20-minute slot.\n\n**Materials:** greeting dialogue video; time-of-day graphic (morning/afternoon/evening); frames slide.\n\n**Lesson flow:**\n\n| # | Phase | Content | ~Time |\n|--|--|--|--|\n| 1 | Warm-up / recall | Recall Unit 0: say your name + one survival phrase. | 3 min |\n| 2 | Input | Watch 2 short greeting dialogues; text + Arabic gloss; note *\"How are you?\"* | 6 min |\n| 3 | Notice | Explain (Arabic) which greeting for which time; *\"How are you?\"* + answers. | 5 min |\n| 4 | Accent Lab | Drill /h/ (hello, how) — don't drop it; friendly intonation of *\"How are you?\"* | 4 min |\n| 5 | Guided output | Pair frames: A: *\"Hi, how are you?\"* B: *\"I'm fine, thanks. And you?\"* (swap roles) | 6 min |\n| 6 | Free output | Live/pairs: greet 2 classmates properly for the current time of day; record one. | 4 min |\n| 7 | Feedback | Compare to model; check the /h/ isn't dropped. | 1 min |\n| 8 | Assignment | Greet someone in English today; post a voice note: greeting + *\"How are you?\"* + answer. | 1 min |\n\n**Differentiation:** Support — one greeting (hi) + one answer (fine). Stretch — add *\"Nice to meet you.\"*\n**Success check:** learner performs a full greeting exchange intelligibly. **Queue:** greetings recycle in every warm-up; *to be* enters queue.\n**Coach notes:** Arabic speakers sometimes drop /h/ or use only \"hello\" — encourage time-appropriate greetings and keep it warm.\n\n---\n\n## S0-U1-L02 — \"Hello, I'm…\" — Saying Who You Are\n\n*(This is the fully worked example in `curriculum/02-lesson-template.md` Part B — reproduced here as the unit's L02 so the unit file is complete.)*\n\n**Metadata:** Stage 0 / Pre-A1→A1 · Unit 1 · ~30 min · recorded + live · prerequisites: L01.\n\n**Objectives (can-do):**\n- The learner can introduce themselves with name, country, and job in simple sentences.\n- The learner can ask *\"What's your name?\"* and respond.\n- The learner can produce their first spoken self-introduction on video.\n\n**Target language:** name/country/job; *\"My name is… / I'm… / I'm from… / I'm a/an + job.\"*\n**Grammar:** *to be* (I'm/you're). **Accent Lab focus:** /aɪ/ in \"I\", clear /m/ in \"I'm\"; stress the key word (*\"I'm a **teacher**\"*).\n**Arabic support:** ~70%. **Mindset focus:** consistency > intensity.\n\n**Materials:** 3 self-introduction clips (American models); frames slide (*My name is ___ / I'm from ___ / I'm a ___*); model audio \"I / I'm / I'm a teacher\"; recording prompt; Google Form.\n\n**Lesson flow:**\n\n| # | Phase | Content | ~Time |\n|--|--|--|--|\n| 1 | Warm-up / recall | Recall greetings (L01): say hello + how are you. | 3 min |\n| 2 | Input | Watch 3 short self-introductions; meaning supported with text + Arabic gloss. | 7 min |\n| 3 | Notice | Highlight *\"My name is… / I'm from… / I'm a…\"*; brief Arabic explanation of *to be*. | 5 min |\n| 4 | Accent Lab | Drill *\"I → I'm → I'm a teacher\"* (/aɪ/ + /m/); record-and-compare. | 4 min |\n| 5 | Guided output | Fill frames aloud with own info; substitution (swap country/job). | 6 min |\n| 6 | Free output | Record a 3-sentence self-introduction (real info). | 3 min |\n| 7 | Feedback | Compare to model; note 1 thing to improve; keep the recording. | 2 min |\n| 8 | Assignment | Post a 20–30s self-introduction voice note in the community. | — |\n\n**Differentiation:** Support — full written frames + audio; allow re-listening. Stretch — add *\"I live in…\"*.\n**Success check:** 3 correct, intelligible self-intro sentences (rubric: Accuracy + Pronunciation + Fluency at A1 threshold). **Queue:** *to be*, \"I'm from…\", jobs → return L03 warm-up, ~3 days, ~1 week.\n**Coach notes:** expect dropped \"am/is\" (\"I teacher\") — correct the *to be* target now; note other errors for later (Handbook §11).\n\n---\n\n## S0-U1-L03 — \"Where Are You From?\" — Countries & Nationalities\n\n**Metadata:** Stage 0 / A1 · Unit 1 · ~30 min · recorded + live · prerequisites: L02.\n\n**Objectives (can-do):**\n- The learner can ask and answer *\"Where are you from?\"* / *\"I'm from ___.\"*\n- The learner can name several countries and a few nationalities (basic).\n- The learner can ask a classmate and report (*\"He's from Egypt.\"*).\n\n**Target language:** countries (Egypt, Saudi Arabia, UAE, England, America…); *\"Where are you from?\"*;\n*\"I'm from ___.\"*; intro to nationality (Egyptian). **Grammar:** *to be* extended (he's/she's from).\n**Accent Lab focus:** clear /r/ (from, are); stress on the country name.\n**Arabic support:** ~60% (starting to fade). **Mindset focus:** review your goal — are you practicing daily?\n\n**Materials:** map/flags visual; country flashcards; dialogue clip; frames slide; Google Form.\n\n**Lesson flow:**\n\n| # | Phase | Content | ~Time |\n|--|--|--|--|\n| 1 | Warm-up / recall | Retrieval: say your L02 self-intro (name + job). | 4 min |\n| 2 | Input | Clip: people saying where they're from; text + gloss. | 6 min |\n| 3 | Notice | *\"Where are you from?\"* → *\"I'm from ___.\"*; add *he's/she's from*; Egyptian (nationality) intro. | 6 min |\n| 4 | Accent Lab | /r/ in *from/are*; stress the country (*\"I'm from **E**gypt\"*). | 4 min |\n| 5 | Guided output | Substitution with flashcards: swap countries; then *\"He's from ___.\"* | 5 min |\n| 6 | Free output | Live/pairs: ask 2 classmates *\"Where are you from?\"*, then report to the group; record. | 4 min |\n| 7 | Feedback | Check /r/ and *to be*; note anything for the queue. | 1 min |\n| 8 | Assignment | Post: *\"Hi, I'm ___. I'm from ___.\"* + tag where a classmate is from. | — |\n\n**Differentiation:** Support — just *\"I'm from ___.\"* Stretch — *\"I'm Egyptian, from Cairo.\"*\n**Success check:** correct question + answer with clear country stress. **Queue:** he's/she's + from recycle in U2.\n**Coach notes:** don't overload nationalities (they're irregular) — one is enough at A1; focus on the pattern.\n\n---\n\n## S0-U1-L04 — \"What Do You Do?\" — Jobs\n\n**Metadata:** Stage 0 / A1 · Unit 1 · ~30 min · recorded + live · prerequisites: L03.\n\n**Objectives (can-do):**\n- The learner can ask and answer *\"What do you do?\"* / *\"I'm a/an ___.\"*\n- The learner can name common jobs and use **a/an** correctly.\n- The learner can talk about someone else's job (*\"She's a doctor.\"*).\n\n**Target language:** jobs (teacher, engineer, doctor, student, nurse, driver, accountant…); *\"What do you do?\"*;\n*\"I'm a/an ___.\"* **Grammar:** *to be* + a/an (article before jobs); he's/she's a…\n**Accent Lab focus:** a vs an (*an engineer*); stress on the job word.\n**Arabic support:** ~60%. **Mindset focus:** small wins compound — you can already introduce yourself!\n\n**Materials:** job flashcards; dialogue clip; a/an rule card; frames slide; Google Form.\n\n**Lesson flow:**\n\n| # | Phase | Content | ~Time |\n|--|--|--|--|\n| 1 | Warm-up / recall | Retrieval: *\"I'm ___, I'm from ___.\"* (L02–L03). | 4 min |\n| 2 | Input | Clip: people saying their jobs; text + gloss. | 6 min |\n| 3 | Notice | *\"What do you do?\"* → *\"I'm a/an ___.\"*; explain (Arabic) **a** before consonant, **an** before vowel sound. | 6 min |\n| 4 | Accent Lab | Drill *\"a teacher / an engineer\"*; stress the job. | 4 min |\n| 5 | Guided output | Flashcard substitution; then *\"He's a driver / She's a doctor.\"* | 5 min |\n| 6 | Free output | Live/pairs: ask *\"What do you do?\"*, answer, report; record. | 4 min |\n| 7 | Feedback | Check a/an and the *to be*; queue any weak sounds. | 1 min |\n| 8 | Assignment | Post: *\"I'm a/an ___.\"* + one classmate's job. | — |\n\n**Differentiation:** Support — provide the a/an card. Stretch — *\"I'm a student and I want to be an engineer.\"* (preview want to)\n**Success check:** correct *\"I'm a/an + job\"* with right article. **Queue:** a/an + jobs recycle in U3 (a/an with objects).\n**Coach notes:** common error: omitting the article (\"I engineer\") — this is the lesson to fix it gently and repeatedly.\n\n---\n\n## S0-U1-L05 — Unit Speaking Task: Introduce Yourself (+ Review)\n\n**Metadata:** Stage 0 / A1 · Unit 1 · ~30 min · recorded + live (assessment-style) · prerequisites: L01–L04.\n\n**Objectives (can-do):**\n- The learner can give a connected self-introduction: greeting + name + country + job (4–6 sentences).\n- The learner can ask another person the same questions.\n- The learner records their **Unit-1 self-introduction** (a visible milestone vs the Day-1 clip).\n\n**Target language:** integrated Unit-1 language. **Grammar:** *to be* consolidated.\n**Accent Lab focus:** overall clarity — /aɪ/, /r/, a/an, stress; each learner's flagged weak sound.\n**Arabic support:** ~50% (fading). **Mindset focus:** notice how far you've come in ~2 weeks.\n\n**Materials:** a model 5-sentence self-introduction; a simple checklist card; recording prompt; Unit-1 quiz (Google Form).\n\n**Lesson flow:**\n\n| # | Phase | Content | ~Time |\n|--|--|--|--|\n| 1 | Warm-up / recall (retrieval-heavy) | Rapid recall: greeting, name, from, job — as a chain. | 5 min |\n| 2 | Input/model | Watch a clean model self-introduction (5 sentences). | 4 min |\n| 3 | Notice | Break the model into the frames learned; the \"checklist\" of a good intro. | 4 min |\n| 4 | Accent Lab clinic | Each learner drills their **one** weakest sound flagged in U0–U1. | 4 min |\n| 5 | Guided output | Rehearse the full intro with the checklist card; peer practice (pairs). | 6 min |\n| 6 | Free output (task) | Record the full self-introduction (greeting + name + from + job + one extra). | 4 min |\n| 7 | Feedback | Self + peer check against the checklist; coach gives group-level delayed feedback. | 2 min |\n| 8 | Assignment/queue | Post the self-intro video/voice note; complete Unit-1 quiz; celebrate the milestone. | 1 min |\n\n**Differentiation:** Support — 4 sentences with the card. Stretch — add *\"I live in ___\"* / *\"Nice to meet you.\"*\n**Success check (formative summary of Unit 1):** connected 4–6 sentence self-intro, intelligible, correct *to be* and a/an (rubric: Fluency + Accuracy + Pronunciation at A1). **Queue:** whole self-intro recycles into Unit 2 warm-ups and Unit 10 review.\n**Coach notes:** This recording is a key proof asset — with consent, it pairs beautifully with the graduation clip later. Make the milestone feel big.\n\n---\n\n## Unit 1 — end-of-unit check (formative)\n\n- **Quiz (Google Form):** to-be forms; a/an choice; match question → answer (What do you do? / Where are you from?).\n- **Speaking task:** the L05 self-introduction, scored on the rubric (A1 threshold).\n- **Feeds:** Cohort Tracker weekly activity + pronunciation notes; flags each learner's weak sound for the queue.\n\n---\n\n*Next: Unit 2 — Family & People (L01–L05). Then continue unit by unit per the map (build just ahead of\nthe founding cohort).*\n","u10":"# Stage 0 · Unit 10 — Putting It Together (full lessons, v1.0)\n\n> **Spec task:** 1.3 (final Stage-0 unit) · **Built on:** `curriculum/02-lesson-template.md` ·\n> **Map:** `stage0/00-stage0-unit-map.md` · **Assessment:** links to Task 1.6 (assessment suite)\n> **Arabic support:** ~15–20% (near immersion; brief Arabic only for reassurance/instructions).\n> **Unit goal:** consolidate all of Stage 0 through retrieval, fix each learner's top pronunciation\n> issues, rehearse the A1 conversation, and complete the **summative assessment + graduation \"after\"\n> speaking sample** that proves the transformation from Day 1.\n\n**Unit lessons:** L01 Big review 1 · L02 Big review 2 · L03 Accent clinic (top 3 sounds) ·\nL04 Practice conversation (A1 mock) · L05 Stage-0 summative + graduation sample.\n\n**Assets to produce:** review game boards/prompts, a full A1 conversation model, the summative speaking\nprompt + the 5-dimension rubric sheet (from Task 1.6), the standard graduation-sample prompt (same as the\nDay-1 prompt, for a fair before/after), a Stage-0 completion certificate template (brand-compliant).\n\n---\n\n## S0-U10-L01 — Big Review 1: Identity, Family, Things, Routine\n\n**Metadata:** Stage 0 / A1 · Unit 10 · ~30 min · recorded + live · prerequisites: Units 0–9.\n\n**Objectives (can-do):**\n- The learner can retrieve and reuse language from Units 1–4 (self-intro, family, objects/places, routine).\n- The learner can speak for ~1 minute combining these topics.\n- The learner can self-correct common errors (to-be, a/an, he/she -s, there is/are).\n\n**Target language:** integrated review (Units 1–4). **Grammar:** to be, possessives, have/has, there is/are,\npresent simple + time/frequency. **Accent Lab focus:** rapid recall of earlier targets (/p/–/b/, /θ/–/ð/,\nplural/3rd-person -s). **Arabic support:** ~15%. **Mindset focus:** see how much you can already say — proof of progress.\n\n**Materials:** retrieval prompt cards; a \"talk for a minute\" timer; error self-check card; Google Form (mixed review).\n\n**Lesson flow:**\n\n| # | Phase | Content | ~Time |\n|--|--|--|--|\n| 1 | Warm-up / recall | Rapid-fire retrieval: name, from, job, family count, one routine sentence. | 5 min |\n| 2 | Input/model | Short model that weaves Units 1–4 together (self-intro → family → routine). | 4 min |\n| 3 | Notice | The self-check card: the 4 errors to watch (to-be, a/an, he/she -s, there is/are). | 4 min |\n| 4 | Accent Lab | Quick recall drill of earlier sounds; each learner flags their weakest. | 4 min |\n| 5 | Guided output | Card-prompted mini-talks across the 4 topics; peers catch errors kindly. | 7 min |\n| 6 | Free output | \"Talk for 1 minute about you + your family + your day\"; record. | 4 min |\n| 7 | Feedback | Self + peer check with the card; coach group-level notes. | 2 min |\n| 8 | Assignment | Post the 1-minute talk; complete the mixed-review form. | — |\n\n**Differentiation:** Support — prompts + 45 seconds. Stretch — 90 seconds, no card.\n**Success check:** learner sustains ~1 minute across Units 1–4 topics. **Queue:** flags weak areas for L03 accent clinic + L04 mock.\n**Coach notes:** this is retrieval practice (the testing effect) — resist re-teaching; let them recall and self-correct. Note each learner's recurring errors for the clinic.\n\n---\n\n## S0-U10-L02 — Big Review 2: Free Time, Food, Description, Directions, Now\n\n**Metadata:** Stage 0 / A1 · Unit 10 · ~30 min · recorded + live · prerequisites: L01.\n\n**Objectives (can-do):**\n- The learner can retrieve and reuse language from Units 5–9 (free time, food/shopping, description, directions, now).\n- The learner can perform short functional exchanges (order, ask directions, describe, say what's happening).\n- The learner can keep a conversation going with questions and follow-ups.\n\n**Target language:** integrated review (Units 5–9). **Grammar:** like + -ing, can, would like, adjective+noun,\nhave got, imperatives, present continuous. **Accent Lab focus:** recall of -ing, /l/–/r/, clusters, intonation.\n**Arabic support:** ~15%. **Mindset focus:** these are real-life skills — you can function in English now.\n\n**Materials:** function-station prompts (café, street, describe-a-person, scene); Google Form (mixed review).\n\n**Lesson flow:**\n\n| # | Phase | Content | ~Time |\n|--|--|--|--|\n| 1 | Warm-up / recall | Rapid retrieval: a like, a can/can't, a \"how much\", a direction, a \"right now\". | 5 min |\n| 2 | Input/model | A model that chains Units 5–9 functions in a short scene. | 4 min |\n| 3 | Notice | The \"keep it going\" toolkit: questions + follow-ups (Do you like…?, What are you doing?). | 4 min |\n| 4 | Accent Lab | Recall drill: -ing, /l/–/r/, clusters; each learner targets their weakest. | 4 min |\n| 5 | Guided output | Rotate function stations (order / directions / describe / scene) in pairs. | 7 min |\n| 6 | Free output | Pick 2 functions and perform them in a short exchange; record. | 4 min |\n| 7 | Feedback | Self + peer check; coach group-level notes. | 2 min |\n| 8 | Assignment | Post one function exchange; complete the mixed-review form. | — |\n\n**Differentiation:** Support — 1 station with prompts. Stretch — 3 stations, no prompts, with follow-ups.\n**Success check:** learner performs ≥2 functional exchanges intelligibly with interaction. **Queue:** flags weak areas for L03 + L04.\n**Coach notes:** emphasize conversation management (survival phrases + questions). This is where \"functional A1\" becomes visible.\n\n---\n\n## S0-U10-L03 — Accent Clinic: Fix Your Top 3 Sounds\n\n**Metadata:** Stage 0 / A1 · Unit 10 · ~30 min · recorded + live · prerequisites: L01–L02.\n\n**Objectives (can-do):**\n- The learner can identify their 3 weakest sounds (from the queue flags across Units 0–9).\n- The learner can apply the fix technique for each (articulation cue + minimal pairs + record-and-compare).\n- The learner can produce those sounds more clearly in words and a sentence.\n\n**Target language:** the learner's personal target sounds (commonly /p/–/b/, /v/–/f/, /θ/–/ð/, clusters,\n-ing, /l/–/r/). **Accent Lab focus:** personalized — each learner's flagged three. **Arabic support:** ~15%.\n**Mindset focus:** targeted practice on weaknesses = deliberate practice; this is how accents improve.\n\n**Materials:** each learner's weak-sound flags (from the Cohort Tracker); the Accent Lab technique cards;\nminimal-pair audio bank; record-and-compare tool.\n\n**Lesson flow:**\n\n| # | Phase | Content | ~Time |\n|--|--|--|--|\n| 1 | Warm-up / recall | Learner reviews their flagged sounds from Units 0–9. | 4 min |\n| 2 | Input | Listen to the model for each target sound (minimal pairs). | 5 min |\n| 3 | Notice | Re-teach the articulation cue for each of the learner's 3 sounds (mouth position). | 5 min |\n| 4 | Accent Lab (core) | Cycle each sound: perceive → produce in isolation → words → phrase; record-and-compare. | 9 min |\n| 5 | Guided output | Put all 3 sounds into one sentence each; refine. | 4 min |\n| 6 | Free output | Record a short paragraph loaded with the 3 target sounds. | 2 min |\n| 7 | Feedback | Compare to Day-1/earlier samples; note audible improvement. | 1 min |\n| 8 | Assignment | Daily 5-min drill on the 3 sounds before the summative. | — |\n\n**Differentiation:** Support — 2 sounds, more listening. Stretch — 3 sounds + connected-speech/prosody polish.\n**Success check:** measurable clarity improvement on the learner's 3 target sounds. **Queue:** these sounds carry into Stage 1.\n**Coach notes:** personalization is the point — use each learner's actual data. Celebrate audible gains; this directly improves the Pronunciation rubric score on the summative.\n\n---\n\n## S0-U10-L04 — Practice Conversation: The A1 Exchange (Mock)\n\n**Metadata:** Stage 0 / A1 · Unit 10 · ~30 min · recorded + live (mock assessment) · prerequisites: L01–L03.\n\n**Objectives (can-do):**\n- The learner can sustain the full A1 conversation (greet → self-intro → family → routine → free time →\n  a functional exchange → close) with a partner.\n- The learner can ask and answer questions naturally, using survival phrases when needed.\n- The learner is ready (and calm) for the summative in L05.\n\n**Target language:** all Stage-0 language. **Grammar:** integrated. **Accent Lab focus:** overall clarity +\nthe learner's 3 target sounds. **Arabic support:** ~10% (near full immersion). **Mindset focus:** exam calm —\nit's a conversation, not a test; you've done this all term.\n\n**Materials:** the A1 conversation model + question bank; the 5-dimension rubric sheet (so learners know\nthe criteria); recording setup.\n\n**Lesson flow:**\n\n| # | Phase | Content | ~Time |\n|--|--|--|--|\n| 1 | Warm-up / recall | Quick full self-intro + one function (recall). | 4 min |\n| 2 | Input/model | Watch a full A1 conversation model; point out the rubric dimensions in action. | 5 min |\n| 3 | Notice | Walk through the rubric (Fluency/Accuracy/Pronunciation/Range/Interaction) in plain terms. | 4 min |\n| 4 | Accent Lab | Final quick drill of the learner's 3 target sounds. | 3 min |\n| 5 | Guided output | Full mock conversation in pairs; coach observes, gives delayed feedback. | 8 min |\n| 6 | Free output | Second run, aiming to improve one rubric dimension; record. | 4 min |\n| 7 | Feedback | Rubric-based feedback (strengths + one focus for the real thing). | 2 min |\n| 8 | Assignment | Light rehearsal + rest; the summative is next session. | — |\n\n**Differentiation:** Support — shorter exchange with a question bank. Stretch — longer, spontaneous, no bank.\n**Success check:** learner completes the full A1 exchange; knows the rubric. **Queue:** none — this preps the summative.\n**Coach notes:** reduce anxiety (affective filter) — frame the summative as a friendly conversation. Give each learner one concrete, encouraging focus point.\n\n---\n\n## S0-U10-L05 — Stage-0 Summative Assessment + Graduation \"After\" Sample\n\n**Metadata:** Stage 0 / A1 · Unit 10 · ~30–40 min · live (or recorded) assessment · prerequisites: L01–L04.\n\n**Objectives (can-do):**\n- The learner demonstrates the **A1 exit criteria** (per `curriculum/01-cefr-roadmap.md` §3).\n- The learner completes the Stage-0 summative (quiz + speaking task on the 5-dimension rubric).\n- The learner records the **graduation \"after\" speaking sample** on the **same prompt as the Day-1\n  sample**, producing the before/after proof pair.\n\n**Assessment components:**\n1. **Written/recognition quiz (Google Form):** a mixed Stage-0 check (to-be, a/an, plurals, present simple\n   incl. he/she -s, there is/are, some/any, can, would like, adjective order, present continuous,\n   now-vs-usually, prices, directions vocab).\n2. **Speaking task (the A1 exchange):** greet → self-introduce → talk about family, routine, free time →\n   one functional exchange (order / directions) → describe what's happening now → close. ~5–8 minutes.\n3. **Graduation sample:** the learner records the **standard prompt** (identical to Day-1) — e.g.,\n   *\"Tell me about yourself, your family, and your typical day.\"* — for the before/after pair.\n\n**Scoring (5-dimension rubric, 0–5 each — from Handbook §16 / Task 1.6):**\nFluency · Accuracy · Pronunciation/Accent · Range · Interaction. **A1 pass threshold:** a defined minimum\nacross dimensions (finalized in the assessment suite, Task 1.6).\n\n**Flow:**\n\n| # | Phase | Content | ~Time |\n|--|--|--|--|\n| 1 | Settle | Brief, warm reassurance (near-English); it's a conversation. | 2 min |\n| 2 | Quiz | Learner completes the recognition quiz (Google Form). | 8 min |\n| 3 | Speaking task | The A1 exchange with the coach (or paired + coach-observed); scored on the rubric. | 8–12 min |\n| 4 | Graduation sample | Record the standard prompt for the before/after pair. | 3 min |\n| 5 | Result & feedback | Coach gives the CEFR determination + warm, specific feedback + next-stage preview. | 4 min |\n| 6 | Celebrate & log | Issue the Stage-0 certificate; log everything in the Cohort Tracker (with consent). | — |\n\n**Recording in the Cohort Tracker (Task 0.5 fields):** `end_level`, `final_speaking_score`, `cefr_gain`,\n`graduation_sample_link`, and (with consent) prepare the paired Day-1↔graduation clip for testimonials.\n\n**Success check (Stage-0 completion):** learner meets the A1 exit criteria and has a documented CEFR gain\nfrom the Day-1 baseline. **Next:** progress to Stage 1 (Elementary / A2) or the community for continued practice.\n**Coach notes:** This is the proof moment the whole strategy is built around — the paired before/after clip\nis your strongest marketing asset. Make graduation feel like the achievement it is: from zero to a real\nEnglish conversation in ~one term.\n\n---\n\n## Unit 10 / Stage 0 — completion checklist\n\n- [ ] Every learner has a Day-1 sample **and** a graduation sample on the same prompt.\n- [ ] Summative quiz + speaking task scored on the rubric; CEFR (A1) determination recorded.\n- [ ] Cohort Tracker updated (end level, scores, CEFR gain, links, consent, membership interest).\n- [ ] Stage-0 certificate issued (brand-compliant).\n- [ ] Testimonials/before-after pairs prepared (with consent) for the proof flywheel.\n- [ ] Learners guided to their next step (Stage 1 or community).\n\n---\n\n## 🎓 Stage 0 — Foundations: COMPLETE\n\nWith Unit 10 authored, the **entire Stage-0 Foundations curriculum (Units 0–10, ~55 lessons, Pre-A1→A1)\nis finished** — a full, teachable, assessment-backed level that takes an absolute beginner from zero to a\nreal A1 English conversation, with before/after proof built in.\n\n*Next options (per the plan): Task 1.4 (Accent Lab syllabus — systematize the pronunciation thread),\nTask 1.6 (assessment suite — formalize the rubric/placement referenced throughout), Task 1.5 (mindset\ntrack), or move toward launch (Phase 3 — the Founding Cohort Offer).*\n","u2":"# Stage 0 · Unit 2 — Family & People (full lessons, v1.0)\n\n> **Spec task:** 1.3 · **Built on:** `curriculum/02-lesson-template.md` · **Map:** `stage0/00-stage0-unit-map.md`\n> **Arabic support:** ~60% (fading), instructions/explanations in Egyptian Arabic; learner outputs in English.\n> **Unit goal:** by the end, the learner can talk about their family — say who people are, use my/your/his/her,\n> say what family they have, and give ages — and can describe their family in a short recorded piece.\n> **Grammar spine:** possessive adjectives (my/your/his/her) + possessive 's · this/that/these/those ·\n> have/has · **Accent Lab:** /θ/–/ð/ (this / these / mother / father) + plural & possessive -s sounds ·\n> **Mindset:** overcoming fear and embarrassment of speaking.\n\n**Unit lessons:** L01 My family · L02 \"This is my…\" (his/her) · L03 have/has · L04 Ages & numbers 20–100 ·\nL05 Unit speaking task: describe your family + review.\n\n**Assets to produce:** a family-tree visual, family photos/flashcards, a short \"meet my family\" clip\n(American model), /θ/–/ð/ minimal-pair audio, numbers 20–100 audio, a Google Form per lesson, community\nvoice-note prompts.\n\n---\n\n## S0-U2-L01 — My Family\n\n**Metadata:** Stage 0 / A1 · Unit 2 · ~30 min · recorded + live · prerequisites: Unit 1.\n\n**Objectives (can-do):**\n- The learner can name core family members (mother, father, brother, sister, son, daughter, husband, wife).\n- The learner can use **my** and **your** with family words (*\"my mother\", \"your brother\"*).\n- The learner can say who is in their family (*\"I have a big/small family.\"*).\n\n**Target language:** family vocab; my/your; big/small family. **Grammar:** possessive adjectives my/your.\n**Accent Lab focus:** /ð/ in *mother/father/brother*; /θ/ preview.\n**Arabic support:** ~60%. **Mindset focus:** it's normal to feel shy — speaking anyway is the skill.\n\n**Materials:** family-tree visual; family flashcards; short clip; frames slide; Google Form (label the family tree).\n\n**Lesson flow:**\n\n| # | Phase | Content | ~Time |\n|--|--|--|--|\n| 1 | Warm-up / recall | Retrieval: quick self-intro from U1 (name, from, job). | 4 min |\n| 2 | Input | Clip: someone shows a family photo and names people; text + Arabic gloss. | 6 min |\n| 3 | Notice | Present family words on the tree; *my / your*; explain (Arabic) possessive meaning. | 6 min |\n| 4 | Accent Lab | Drill /ð/ in *mother, father, brother* (tongue tip lightly between teeth + voice). | 4 min |\n| 5 | Guided output | Point to the tree: *\"This is my mother. This is my brother.\"* (my); ask *\"Is this your sister?\"* (your) | 5 min |\n| 6 | Free output | Live/pairs: name 3 people in your real family; record. | 4 min |\n| 7 | Feedback | Check /ð/ and *my/your*; queue weak items. | — |\n| 8 | Assignment | Post a voice note naming 3 family members (*\"my ___\"*). Family-tree quiz form. | 1 min |\n\n**Differentiation:** Support — 3 core words (mother/father/brother). Stretch — add cousin/uncle/aunt.\n**Success check:** learner names ≥3 family members with *my* intelligibly. **Queue:** family words + my/your recycle in L02–L05 and Unit 10.\n**Coach notes:** /θ/ and /ð/ don't exist in the same way in Arabic — expect /s/ or /d/ substitutions (*\"broder\", \"moder\"*). Introduce gently now; it's the unit's Accent Lab thread.\n\n---\n\n## S0-U2-L02 — \"This Is My…\" — Introducing People (his/her)\n\n**Metadata:** Stage 0 / A1 · Unit 2 · ~30 min · recorded + live · prerequisites: L01.\n\n**Objectives (can-do):**\n- The learner can introduce another person (*\"This is my friend, Ali.\"*).\n- The learner can use **his** and **her** correctly (*\"his sister\", \"her job\"*).\n- The learner can use **this/that** for near/far.\n\n**Target language:** this/that; his/her; *\"This is ___.\"* **Grammar:** demonstratives this/that; possessives his/her.\n**Accent Lab focus:** /ð/ in *this/that*; /h/ in *his/her*.\n**Arabic support:** ~60%. **Mindset focus:** speaking about others lowers the spotlight — a comfort trick.\n\n**Materials:** photos of people; this/that visual (near/far); clip; frames slide; Google Form.\n\n**Lesson flow:**\n\n| # | Phase | Content | ~Time |\n|--|--|--|--|\n| 1 | Warm-up / recall | Retrieval: name 2 family members with *my* (L01). | 4 min |\n| 2 | Input | Clip: \"This is my friend… his name is… her job is…\"; text + gloss. | 6 min |\n| 3 | Notice | this (near) vs that (far); his (male) vs her (female); explain (Arabic) the his/her gender logic (based on the owner). | 6 min |\n| 4 | Accent Lab | /ð/ in *this/that*; /h/ not dropped in *his/her*. | 4 min |\n| 5 | Guided output | Introduce photo people: *\"This is my brother. His name is ___.\"* / *\"This is my sister. Her name is ___.\"* | 6 min |\n| 6 | Free output | Live/pairs: introduce a real person (family/friend) using this + his/her; record. | 3 min |\n| 7 | Feedback | Check his/her accuracy (common error) + /ð/. | — |\n| 8 | Assignment | Post: introduce one person — *\"This is my ___. His/Her name is ___.\"* | 1 min |\n\n**Differentiation:** Support — one person, just *\"This is my ___.\"* Stretch — add job: *\"He's a ___.\"*\n**Success check:** correct this + his/her in an introduction. **Queue:** his/her recycle in L03 and later units.\n**Coach notes:** Arabic maps possessives differently — expect his/her mix-ups. Drill: gender follows the **owner** (her brother if the owner is female). Be patient; it recurs.\n\n---\n\n## S0-U2-L03 — have / has: \"I Have Two Brothers\"\n\n**Metadata:** Stage 0 / A1 · Unit 2 · ~30 min · recorded + live · prerequisites: L02.\n\n**Objectives (can-do):**\n- The learner can say what family they have using **have/has** (*\"I have two sisters.\", \"She has a son.\"*).\n- The learner can use plural family nouns with the correct **-s sound**.\n- The learner can ask *\"Do you have…?\"* (basic).\n\n**Target language:** have/has; plurals of family nouns; numbers 1–10 with people.\n**Grammar:** have (I/you/we/they) vs has (he/she/it); plural -s.\n**Accent Lab focus:** plural -s sounds (/s/,/z/,/ɪz/): brother**s** /z/, sister**s** /z/, aunt**s** /s/.\n**Arabic support:** ~60%. **Mindset focus:** mistakes are data — keep going, don't freeze.\n\n**Materials:** number+family combos; clip; plural-sound audio; frames slide; Google Form.\n\n**Lesson flow:**\n\n| # | Phase | Content | ~Time |\n|--|--|--|--|\n| 1 | Warm-up / recall | Retrieval: introduce a person with this + his/her (L02). | 4 min |\n| 2 | Input | Clip: \"I have two brothers and one sister. She has a big family.\"; text + gloss. | 6 min |\n| 3 | Notice | have vs has (he/she → has); plural nouns; explain (Arabic) the he/she = has rule. | 6 min |\n| 4 | Accent Lab | Plural -s endings: brothers/sisters (/z/) vs aunts (/s/); no extra vowel. | 5 min |\n| 5 | Guided output | Substitution: *\"I have ___ brother(s)/sister(s).\"*; then *\"He has ___.\"* | 5 min |\n| 6 | Free output | Live/pairs: say your real family numbers; ask *\"Do you have brothers?\"*; record. | 3 min |\n| 7 | Feedback | Check has (3rd person) + plural -s; queue weak items. | — |\n| 8 | Assignment | Post: *\"I have ___ brother(s) and ___ sister(s).\"* | 1 min |\n\n**Differentiation:** Support — only *\"I have…\"*. Stretch — *\"My brother has a son.\"* (has + family)\n**Success check:** correct have/has + intelligible plural -s. **Queue:** has + plurals recycle into U3 (there is/are + plurals).\n**Coach notes:** two classic errors here — \"he have\" and adding a vowel before -s (\"brotheres\"). Target both; they generalize across the whole course.\n\n---\n\n## S0-U2-L04 — Ages & Numbers 20–100\n\n**Metadata:** Stage 0 / A1 · Unit 2 · ~30 min · recorded · prerequisites: L03.\n\n**Objectives (can-do):**\n- The learner can say numbers 20–100 (twenty, thirty… hundred).\n- The learner can ask and give ages (*\"How old are you?\" / \"I'm ___.\" / \"My father is ___.\"*).\n- The learner can distinguish tricky pairs: **-teen vs -ty** (thirteen vs thirty).\n\n**Target language:** 20–100; *\"How old are you?\" / \"I'm ___ (years old).\" / \"He's/She's ___.\"*\n**Grammar:** *to be* for age (I'm ___); numbers. **Accent Lab focus:** **-teen (stressed) vs -ty (unstressed)**\n— thir**teen** vs **thir**ty; /θ/ in thirty/thirteen.\n**Arabic support:** ~55%. **Mindset focus:** you can already say a lot — build on it.\n\n**Materials:** numbers 20–100 audio; -teen/-ty minimal-pair audio; age-frames slide; Google Form (listen → write the age).\n\n**Lesson flow:**\n\n| # | Phase | Content | ~Time |\n|--|--|--|--|\n| 1 | Warm-up / recall | Retrieval: numbers 0–20 (Unit 0) + your family count (L03). | 4 min |\n| 2 | Input | Listen to 20–100 in tens; then ages in context; text + gloss. | 6 min |\n| 3 | Notice | The tens (twenty…ninety, hundred); *\"How old are you?\"* → *\"I'm ___.\"*; the -teen/-ty trap. | 6 min |\n| 4 | Accent Lab | Drill thir**teen** vs **thir**ty (stress + /θ/); fif**teen** vs **fif**ty. | 5 min |\n| 5 | Guided output | Say your age; ages of 2 family members (*\"My mother is ___.\"*). | 5 min |\n| 6 | Free output | Record: *\"I'm ___ years old. My ___ is ___.\"* | 3 min |\n| 7 | Feedback | Check -teen/-ty distinction + /θ/. | — |\n| 8 | Assignment | Post a voice note with your age + one family member's age. Numbers quiz form. | 1 min |\n\n**Differentiation:** Support — just your own age. Stretch — ages of 3 people + \"How old is your…?\" question.\n**Success check:** intelligible 20–100 + age statement; audible -teen/-ty difference. **Queue:** numbers 20–100 return in U6 (prices).\n**Coach notes:** the -teen/-ty confusion causes real-world misunderstandings (13 vs 30) — worth over-practicing. Stress is the key cue.\n\n---\n\n## S0-U2-L05 — Unit Speaking Task: Describe Your Family (+ Review)\n\n**Metadata:** Stage 0 / A1 · Unit 2 · ~30 min · recorded + live (assessment-style) · prerequisites: L01–L04.\n\n**Objectives (can-do):**\n- The learner can describe their family in 4–6 connected sentences (who, how many, names, ages).\n- The learner can ask another person about their family.\n- The learner records a **\"my family\"** piece (a growing portfolio of proof).\n\n**Target language:** integrated Unit-2 language. **Grammar:** possessives + have/has + ages consolidated.\n**Accent Lab focus:** /θ/–/ð/ + plural -s + -teen/-ty; each learner's flagged weak sound.\n**Arabic support:** ~50%. **Mindset focus:** notice reduced fear — you're speaking more freely than week 1.\n\n**Materials:** a model \"my family\" description; a checklist card; recording prompt; Unit-2 quiz (Google Form).\n\n**Lesson flow:**\n\n| # | Phase | Content | ~Time |\n|--|--|--|--|\n| 1 | Warm-up / recall (retrieval-heavy) | Chain recall: family words, my/his/her, have/has, ages. | 5 min |\n| 2 | Input/model | Watch a model \"my family\" description (5–6 sentences). | 4 min |\n| 3 | Notice | Break the model into learned frames; build the \"good description\" checklist. | 4 min |\n| 4 | Accent Lab clinic | Each learner drills their one weakest sound flagged in U2. | 4 min |\n| 5 | Guided output | Rehearse the full description with the card; peer practice (pairs). | 6 min |\n| 6 | Free output (task) | Record the family description (who + how many + names + ages). | 4 min |\n| 7 | Feedback | Self + peer check vs checklist; coach group-level delayed feedback. | 2 min |\n| 8 | Assignment/queue | Post the family description; complete Unit-2 quiz; celebrate progress. | 1 min |\n\n**Differentiation:** Support — 4 sentences with the card. Stretch — add each person's job/where they live.\n**Success check (Unit-2 summary):** connected 4–6 sentence family description, intelligible, correct possessives + have/has (rubric: Fluency + Accuracy + Pronunciation at A1). **Queue:** family description recycles into Unit 10 review.\n**Coach notes:** With Units 0–2 done, the founding cohort can start. This \"my family\" clip is another proof asset — log it in the Cohort Tracker with consent.\n\n---\n\n## Unit 2 — end-of-unit check (formative)\n\n- **Quiz (Google Form):** my/your/his/her; have vs has; plural -s; ages (listen → write).\n- **Speaking task:** the L05 family description, scored on the rubric (A1 threshold).\n- **Feeds:** Cohort Tracker weekly activity + pronunciation notes; updates each learner's weak-sound flag.\n\n---\n\n*Milestone: Units 0–2 complete = enough to launch the founding cohort (first ~3 weeks of content).\nNext: Unit 3 — My Things & Places (there is/are, a/an, plurals, prepositions), continuing build-just-ahead.*\n","u3":"# Stage 0 · Unit 3 — My Things & Places (full lessons, v1.0)\n\n> **Spec task:** 1.3 · **Built on:** `curriculum/02-lesson-template.md` · **Map:** `stage0/00-stage0-unit-map.md`\n> **Arabic support:** ~55% (fading), instructions/explanations in Egyptian Arabic; learner outputs in English.\n> **Unit goal:** by the end, the learner can name everyday objects, say what there is in a room or place,\n> and locate things — and can describe their own room in a short recorded piece.\n> **Grammar spine:** a/an/the · regular plurals · there is / there are · prepositions of place\n> (in/on/under/next to/behind/in front of) · **Accent Lab:** consonant clusters *without* vowel insertion\n> (desk, books, next, chairs) · **Mindset:** designing a practice-friendly environment.\n\n**Unit lessons:** L01 Everyday objects & a/an · L02 Plurals (and their sounds) · L03 There is / There are ·\nL04 Where is it? Prepositions of place · L05 Unit speaking task: describe your room + review.\n\n**Assets to produce:** object flashcards, a labelled \"room\" image, a \"my room\" clip (American model),\nconsonant-cluster audio, prepositions visual, a Google Form per lesson, community voice-note prompts.\n\n---\n\n## S0-U3-L01 — Everyday Objects & a/an\n\n**Metadata:** Stage 0 / A1 · Unit 3 · ~30 min · recorded + live · prerequisites: Unit 2.\n\n**Objectives (can-do):**\n- The learner can name common everyday objects (book, pen, phone, table, chair, bag, key, cup…).\n- The learner can use **a/an** correctly with singular objects.\n- The learner can ask *\"What's this?\"* and answer *\"It's a/an ___.\"*\n\n**Target language:** everyday objects; *\"What's this?\" / \"It's a/an ___.\"* **Grammar:** a (consonant sound) / an (vowel sound); it's.\n**Accent Lab focus:** final consonants pronounced clearly (boo**k**, ba**g**, cu**p**) — no added vowel.\n**Arabic support:** ~55%. **Mindset focus:** set up a small \"English corner\" at home (environment cues).\n\n**Materials:** object flashcards; a/an rule card (revisit from U1-L04); clip; frames slide; Google Form.\n\n**Lesson flow:**\n\n| # | Phase | Content | ~Time |\n|--|--|--|--|\n| 1 | Warm-up / recall | Retrieval: a/an + jobs from U1-L04 (\"I'm an engineer\"). | 4 min |\n| 2 | Input | Clip: someone shows objects — \"This is a pen. It's a phone.\"; text + gloss. | 6 min |\n| 3 | Notice | Object words; *\"What's this?\" → \"It's a/an ___.\"*; re-explain a vs an (vowel sound). | 6 min |\n| 4 | Accent Lab | Clear final consonants: book/bag/cup/key — say the last sound, no \"book-uh\". | 4 min |\n| 5 | Guided output | Flashcard drill: *\"What's this?\" \"It's a ___.\"*; include an-words (an apple, an umbrella). | 6 min |\n| 6 | Free output | Live/pairs: hold up 3 real objects near you and name them; record one. | 3 min |\n| 7 | Feedback | Check a/an + clear final consonants; queue weak items. | — |\n| 8 | Assignment | Post a voice note naming 5 objects on your desk (*\"It's a ___.\"*). Objects quiz form. | 1 min |\n\n**Differentiation:** Support — 3 objects with *\"a\"* only. Stretch — mix a/an with 6 objects.\n**Success check:** learner names ≥5 objects with correct a/an. **Queue:** objects + a/an recycle in L02–L05.\n**Coach notes:** Arabic speakers may add a vowel after final consonants (\"booku\") — pair this with the cluster work in L02. Praise clean endings.\n\n---\n\n## S0-U3-L02 — Plurals (and Their Sounds)\n\n**Metadata:** Stage 0 / A1 · Unit 3 · ~30 min · recorded + live · prerequisites: L01.\n\n**Objectives (can-do):**\n- The learner can form regular plurals (book→books, box→boxes) and a few common irregulars (man/men, child/children).\n- The learner can pronounce plural -s as /s/, /z/, /ɪz/ correctly.\n- The learner can say how many objects there are (*\"two books, three chairs\"*).\n\n**Target language:** plurals of Unit-3 objects; numbers + plural nouns. **Grammar:** regular plural rules (+s, +es); key irregulars.\n**Accent Lab focus:** the three plural sounds — book**s** /s/, pen**s** /z/, box**es** /ɪz/ — and **consonant clusters** (desks, books) without inserted vowels.\n**Arabic support:** ~55%. **Mindset focus:** tiny daily reps beat one long session.\n\n**Materials:** plural-sound audio (3 groups); cluster words audio; number+object cards; Google Form (choose the -s sound).\n\n**Lesson flow:**\n\n| # | Phase | Content | ~Time |\n|--|--|--|--|\n| 1 | Warm-up / recall | Retrieval: name 3 objects with a/an (L01). | 4 min |\n| 2 | Input | Listen: \"one book, two books; one box, two boxes\"; text + gloss. | 6 min |\n| 3 | Notice | The +s / +es rule; the 3 sounds (/s/,/z/,/ɪz/); a couple of irregulars (man/men). | 6 min |\n| 4 | Accent Lab | Sort words by -s sound; drill clusters: desk**s**, book**s**, chair**s** — no \"deskuh\". | 6 min |\n| 5 | Guided output | Number + plural: *\"two pens, three books, four boxes.\"* | 5 min |\n| 6 | Free output | Live/pairs: count objects around you (*\"I have two ___ and three ___.\"*); record. | 3 min |\n| 7 | Feedback | Check plural sounds + no vowel insertion; queue weak items. | — |\n| 8 | Assignment | Post: count 5 things around you in plural. Plural-sound quiz form. | 1 min |\n\n**Differentiation:** Support — regular +s only. Stretch — add box/boxes (/ɪz/) + one irregular.\n**Success check:** correct plural forms with intelligible -s sounds. **Queue:** plurals feed there are (L03) and U6 (food quantities).\n**Coach notes:** vowel-insertion in clusters is a signature Arabic-transfer error — this is the unit's core Accent Lab win. Use the \"say the cluster, don't break it\" cue.\n\n---\n\n## S0-U3-L03 — There Is / There Are\n\n**Metadata:** Stage 0 / A1 · Unit 3 · ~30 min · recorded + live · prerequisites: L02.\n\n**Objectives (can-do):**\n- The learner can say what exists in a place using **there is** (singular) and **there are** (plural).\n- The learner can ask *\"Is there a…?\" / \"Are there any…?\"* (basic) and answer.\n- The learner can describe a room's contents (*\"There is a bed. There are two chairs.\"*).\n\n**Target language:** there is/are; rooms & furniture (bed, table, chair, window, door, sofa).\n**Grammar:** there is + singular / there are + plural; Is there…? Are there…?\n**Accent Lab focus:** /ð/ in *there*; weak-form \"there are\" linking.\n**Arabic support:** ~55%. **Mindset focus:** review your goal — is your daily habit sticking?\n\n**Materials:** labelled room image; furniture flashcards; clip; frames slide; Google Form.\n\n**Lesson flow:**\n\n| # | Phase | Content | ~Time |\n|--|--|--|--|\n| 1 | Warm-up / recall | Retrieval: plurals from L02 (say 3 plural nouns with clear -s). | 4 min |\n| 2 | Input | Clip/description of a room: \"There is a bed. There are two windows.\"; text + gloss. | 6 min |\n| 3 | Notice | there is (one) vs there are (many); questions Is there…?/Are there…?; explain (Arabic). | 6 min |\n| 4 | Accent Lab | /ð/ in *there*; link \"there are\" naturally. | 4 min |\n| 5 | Guided output | Look at the room image: make 5 sentences (there is/are); ask *\"Is there a…?\"* | 6 min |\n| 6 | Free output | Live/pairs: describe your real room — 3 sentences; record. | 3 min |\n| 7 | Feedback | Check is/are agreement + /ð/; queue weak items. | — |\n| 8 | Assignment | Post: 3 things in your room using there is/are. | 1 min |\n\n**Differentiation:** Support — there is only (singular). Stretch — add questions + \"there isn't/aren't\".\n**Success check:** correct there is/are agreement in ≥3 sentences. **Queue:** there is/are recycle in L04–L05 and U8 (places in town).\n**Coach notes:** common error: \"there is two chairs\" — target agreement now; it recurs across the course.\n\n---\n\n## S0-U3-L04 — Where Is It? Prepositions of Place\n\n**Metadata:** Stage 0 / A1 · Unit 3 · ~30 min · recorded + live · prerequisites: L03.\n\n**Objectives (can-do):**\n- The learner can use prepositions of place (in, on, under, next to, behind, in front of, between).\n- The learner can ask and answer *\"Where is the ___?\" / \"It's on the ___.\"*\n- The learner can locate objects in a room.\n\n**Target language:** prepositions of place; *\"Where is/are…?\" / \"It's/They're + preposition.\"*\n**Grammar:** preposition phrases; it's/they're + location. **Accent Lab focus:** clear vowels in\n*on/under*; linking \"in front of\".\n**Arabic support:** ~50%. **Mindset focus:** speaking about your real space makes practice easy.\n\n**Materials:** prepositions visual (a box + a ball in different positions); room image; frames slide; Google Form.\n\n**Lesson flow:**\n\n| # | Phase | Content | ~Time |\n|--|--|--|--|\n| 1 | Warm-up / recall | Retrieval: there is/are about the room image (L03). | 4 min |\n| 2 | Input | Clip/visual: \"The keys are on the table. The bag is under the chair.\"; text + gloss. | 6 min |\n| 3 | Notice | Present prepositions with the box+ball visual; *\"Where is…?\" → \"It's ___.\"* | 6 min |\n| 4 | Accent Lab | Clear /ɒ/ in *on*, /ʌ/ in *under*; link \"in-front-of\". | 4 min |\n| 5 | Guided output | Ask/answer about the room image: *\"Where is the lamp?\" \"It's next to the bed.\"* | 6 min |\n| 6 | Free output | Live/pairs: ask where 3 real objects are in your space; answer + record. | 3 min |\n| 7 | Feedback | Check preposition choice + clarity; queue weak items. | — |\n| 8 | Assignment | Post: say where 3 things are in your room (with prepositions). | 1 min |\n\n**Differentiation:** Support — 3 prepositions (in/on/under). Stretch — add between / behind / in front of.\n**Success check:** correct location answers for ≥3 objects. **Queue:** prepositions feed U8 (directions).\n**Coach notes:** learners often overuse \"in\" — contrast in/on/under explicitly with the visual.\n\n---\n\n## S0-U3-L05 — Unit Speaking Task: Describe Your Room (+ Review)\n\n**Metadata:** Stage 0 / A1 · Unit 3 · ~30 min · recorded + live (assessment-style) · prerequisites: L01–L04.\n\n**Objectives (can-do):**\n- The learner can describe their room in 5–7 connected sentences (objects + there is/are + locations).\n- The learner can ask another person about their room.\n- The learner records a **\"my room\"** description (portfolio proof).\n\n**Target language:** integrated Unit-3 language. **Grammar:** a/an + plurals + there is/are + prepositions consolidated.\n**Accent Lab focus:** clusters + plural -s + /ð/ \"there\"; each learner's flagged weak sound.\n**Arabic support:** ~50%. **Mindset focus:** compare to week 1 — you're describing a whole scene now.\n\n**Materials:** a model \"my room\" description; a checklist card; recording prompt; Unit-3 quiz (Google Form).\n\n**Lesson flow:**\n\n| # | Phase | Content | ~Time |\n|--|--|--|--|\n| 1 | Warm-up / recall (retrieval-heavy) | Chain recall: objects (a/an), plurals, there is/are, prepositions. | 5 min |\n| 2 | Input/model | Watch a model \"my room\" description (6 sentences). | 4 min |\n| 3 | Notice | Break the model into learned frames; build the \"good description\" checklist. | 4 min |\n| 4 | Accent Lab clinic | Each learner drills their one weakest sound flagged in U3 (usually a cluster or -s). | 4 min |\n| 5 | Guided output | Rehearse the full room description with the card; peer practice (pairs). | 6 min |\n| 6 | Free output (task) | Record the room description (objects + there is/are + where things are). | 4 min |\n| 7 | Feedback | Self + peer check vs checklist; coach group-level delayed feedback. | 2 min |\n| 8 | Assignment/queue | Post the room description; complete Unit-3 quiz; celebrate progress. | 1 min |\n\n**Differentiation:** Support — 5 sentences with the card. Stretch — add colours/adjectives (preview U7) and how many.\n**Success check (Unit-3 summary):** connected 5–7 sentence room description, intelligible, correct there is/are + prepositions + plural -s (rubric: Fluency + Accuracy + Pronunciation at A1). **Queue:** room description recycles into Unit 10 review.\n**Coach notes:** By now the founding cohort is ~4 weeks in; this \"my room\" clip shows visible growth from the Day-1 sample. Log it (with consent) in the Cohort Tracker.\n\n---\n\n## Unit 3 — end-of-unit check (formative)\n\n- **Quiz (Google Form):** a/an; plural forms + sounds; there is vs there are; prepositions of place.\n- **Speaking task:** the L05 room description, scored on the rubric (A1 threshold).\n- **Feeds:** Cohort Tracker weekly activity + pronunciation notes; updates each learner's weak-sound flag.\n\n---\n\n*Next: Unit 4 — Daily Routine (present simple, time, frequency), continuing build-just-ahead of the cohort.*\n","u4":"# Stage 0 · Unit 4 — Daily Routine (full lessons, v1.0)\n\n> **Spec task:** 1.3 · **Built on:** `curriculum/02-lesson-template.md` · **Map:** `stage0/00-stage0-unit-map.md`\n> **Arabic support:** ~50% (fading), instructions/explanations in Egyptian Arabic; learner outputs in English.\n> **Unit goal:** by the end, the learner can describe their daily routine, tell the time, and say how\n> often they do things — and can record a \"my typical day\" description.\n> **Grammar spine:** present simple (I/you/we/they + he/she/it -s) · telling the time · frequency\n> adverbs (always/usually/sometimes/never) · **Accent Lab:** 3rd-person -s endings (/s/,/z/,/ɪz/) +\n> sentence rhythm/stress · **Mindset:** tracking practice; recovering after a missed day (no guilt spiral).\n\n**Unit lessons:** L01 My day (present simple I/you) · L02 He/she + verb-s · L03 What time is it? ·\nL04 How often? (frequency) · L05 Unit speaking task: describe your typical day + review.\n\n**Assets to produce:** daily-activity flashcards, a clock visual, a \"my day\" clip (American model),\n3rd-person -s audio, a frequency-adverbs scale visual, a Google Form per lesson, community voice-note prompts.\n\n---\n\n## S0-U4-L01 — My Day: Daily Activities (Present Simple I/you)\n\n**Metadata:** Stage 0 / A1 · Unit 4 · ~30 min · recorded + live · prerequisites: Unit 3.\n\n**Objectives (can-do):**\n- The learner can name daily activities (get up, have breakfast, go to work, come home, sleep…).\n- The learner can describe their own routine using present simple (I/you): *\"I get up at… I go to work.\"*\n- The learner can ask *\"What time do you…?\"* (basic).\n\n**Target language:** daily-routine verbs/phrases; *\"I get up / have breakfast / go to work / study / sleep.\"*\n**Grammar:** present simple for I/you/we/they (base form). **Accent Lab focus:** linking verbs in phrases\n(*get-up, go-to*); clear content-word stress.\n**Arabic support:** ~50%. **Mindset focus:** track your practice — a simple daily tick keeps momentum.\n\n**Materials:** daily-activity flashcards (with times); \"my day\" clip; frames slide; Google Form (order the day).\n\n**Lesson flow:**\n\n| # | Phase | Content | ~Time |\n|--|--|--|--|\n| 1 | Warm-up / recall | Retrieval: describe your room in 2 sentences (U3). | 4 min |\n| 2 | Input | Clip: \"I get up at 7. I have breakfast. I go to work.\"; text + Arabic gloss. | 6 min |\n| 3 | Notice | Present the routine verbs in order; explain (Arabic) present simple = habits/every day. | 6 min |\n| 4 | Accent Lab | Link phrases naturally: *get-up, go-to-work*; stress the key word. | 4 min |\n| 5 | Guided output | Sequence your morning: *\"First I…, then I…\"* (base verbs). | 6 min |\n| 6 | Free output | Live/pairs: tell a partner 3 things you do every day; record one. | 3 min |\n| 7 | Feedback | Check base-verb use + linking; queue weak items. | — |\n| 8 | Assignment | Post a voice note: 3 daily activities (*\"I … every day.\"*). Order-the-day quiz form. | 1 min |\n\n**Differentiation:** Support — 3 activities. Stretch — add times (preview L03) and \"then/after that\".\n**Success check:** learner states ≥3 routine activities intelligibly. **Queue:** routine verbs feed L02 (he/she) and L04.\n**Coach notes:** keep to I/you here so learners get fluent before the he/she -s rule (L02). Don't pre-load the -s yet.\n\n---\n\n## S0-U4-L02 — He/She + Verb-s (\"She Works…\")\n\n**Metadata:** Stage 0 / A1 · Unit 4 · ~30 min · recorded + live · prerequisites: L01.\n\n**Objectives (can-do):**\n- The learner can describe someone else's routine using the 3rd-person **-s** (*\"He works. She gets up early.\"*).\n- The learner can pronounce -s endings correctly (/s/, /z/, /ɪz/: works /s/, goes /z/, watches /ɪz/).\n- The learner can ask *\"What does he/she do every day?\"* (basic).\n\n**Target language:** routine verbs in 3rd person; *\"He/She + verb-s.\"* **Grammar:** present simple he/she/it (+s/es).\n**Accent Lab focus:** the 3rd-person -s sounds — the same three sounds as plurals (transfer from U3-L02).\n**Arabic support:** ~50%. **Mindset focus:** missed a day? Just restart today — don't quit the whole thing.\n\n**Materials:** 3rd-person -s audio (3 groups); \"his/her day\" clip; frames slide; Google Form (choose the -s sound).\n\n**Lesson flow:**\n\n| # | Phase | Content | ~Time |\n|--|--|--|--|\n| 1 | Warm-up / recall | Retrieval: your own routine (I) from L01. | 4 min |\n| 2 | Input | Clip: \"My brother gets up at 6. He works in a bank. She watches TV.\"; text + gloss. | 6 min |\n| 3 | Notice | The -s rule for he/she/it; connect to plural -s sounds from U3; explain (Arabic). | 6 min |\n| 4 | Accent Lab | Sort verbs by -s sound: works /s/, goes /z/, watches /ɪz/; drill. | 5 min |\n| 5 | Guided output | Transform: I get up → *\"He gets up.\"*; describe a family member's routine (3 sentences). | 6 min |\n| 6 | Free output | Live/pairs: describe a family member's day (he/she + -s); record. | 3 min |\n| 7 | Feedback | Target the missing -s (very common error) + the -s sound; queue. | — |\n| 8 | Assignment | Post: 3 sentences about someone's daily routine (he/she + verb-s). | 1 min |\n\n**Differentiation:** Support — 2 verbs with clear -s. Stretch — mix I and he/she in one description.\n**Success check:** correct 3rd-person -s in ≥3 sentences with intelligible endings. **Queue:** 3rd-person -s recycles across all later units.\n**Coach notes:** \"He work\" (dropped -s) is one of the most persistent Arabic-transfer errors — target it relentlessly but kindly; it's worth the investment.\n\n---\n\n## S0-U4-L03 — What Time Is It? Telling the Time\n\n**Metadata:** Stage 0 / A1 · Unit 4 · ~30 min · recorded + live · prerequisites: L02.\n\n**Objectives (can-do):**\n- The learner can tell the time (o'clock, half past, quarter past/to, and the digital form 7:15).\n- The learner can ask *\"What time is it?\" / \"What time do you…?\"* and answer.\n- The learner can add times to their routine (*\"I get up at 7 o'clock.\"*).\n\n**Target language:** time expressions; *\"What time is it?\" / \"It's ___.\" / \"at ___.\"*\n**Grammar:** at + time; present simple + time. **Accent Lab focus:** clear /kw/ in *quarter*; linking \"It's\".\n**Arabic support:** ~50%. **Mindset focus:** anchor practice to a fixed time (habit-stacking).\n\n**Materials:** clock visual (analog + digital); time flashcards; frames slide; Google Form (match clock → time).\n\n**Lesson flow:**\n\n| # | Phase | Content | ~Time |\n|--|--|--|--|\n| 1 | Warm-up / recall | Retrieval: numbers 0–20 (U0) + he/she routine (L02). | 4 min |\n| 2 | Input | Listen/watch times said in context: \"It's seven o'clock. It's half past eight.\"; text + gloss. | 6 min |\n| 3 | Notice | o'clock / half past / quarter past / quarter to + digital (7:15 = seven fifteen); *at + time*. | 6 min |\n| 4 | Accent Lab | /kw/ in *quarter*; link *\"it's-seven\"*; clear numbers. | 4 min |\n| 5 | Guided output | Read clocks aloud; then add times to your routine (*\"I go to work at 8.\"*). | 6 min |\n| 6 | Free output | Live/pairs: ask *\"What time do you get up?\"*; answer with a real time; record. | 3 min |\n| 7 | Feedback | Check time forms + at + time; queue weak items. | — |\n| 8 | Assignment | Post: your morning routine with 3 times. Clock quiz form. | 1 min |\n\n**Differentiation:** Support — o'clock + digital only. Stretch — full half/quarter forms + a partner Q&A.\n**Success check:** learner tells ≥3 times and links them to routine. **Queue:** times recycle in U9 and later.\n**Coach notes:** the digital form (seven fifteen) is easiest to start; add half/quarter gradually to avoid overload.\n\n---\n\n## S0-U4-L04 — How Often? Frequency Adverbs\n\n**Metadata:** Stage 0 / A1 · Unit 4 · ~30 min · recorded + live · prerequisites: L03.\n\n**Objectives (can-do):**\n- The learner can use frequency adverbs (always, usually, sometimes, never) with the correct word order.\n- The learner can say how often they do things (*\"I always have breakfast. I never drink coffee.\"*).\n- The learner can ask *\"How often do you…?\"* (basic) and say days of the week.\n\n**Target language:** always/usually/often/sometimes/never; days of the week; *\"How often…?\"*\n**Grammar:** adverb position (before the main verb; after *to be*). **Accent Lab focus:** stress on the\nfrequency adverb; days ending clearly (*-day*).\n**Arabic support:** ~45%. **Mindset focus:** consistency = your own \"always\" — be the person who always practices.\n\n**Materials:** frequency scale visual (always→never); days flashcards; frames slide; Google Form.\n\n**Lesson flow:**\n\n| # | Phase | Content | ~Time |\n|--|--|--|--|\n| 1 | Warm-up / recall | Retrieval: times from L03 + a he/she routine sentence. | 4 min |\n| 2 | Input | Clip: \"I always get up early. I sometimes work on Friday.\"; text + gloss. | 6 min |\n| 3 | Notice | The frequency scale; **word order** (I *usually* get up…; I am *always* late); days of the week. | 6 min |\n| 4 | Accent Lab | Stress the adverb (*\"I ALways…\"*); clear *-day* endings. | 4 min |\n| 5 | Guided output | Add frequency to routine: *\"I always…, I never…\"*; say what you do on which day. | 6 min |\n| 6 | Free output | Live/pairs: *\"How often do you…?\"* → answer with an adverb; record. | 3 min |\n| 7 | Feedback | Check adverb position (common error) + stress; queue. | — |\n| 8 | Assignment | Post: 3 sentences with always/usually/sometimes/never. | 1 min |\n\n**Differentiation:** Support — 2 adverbs (always/never). Stretch — add \"on Mondays / at the weekend\".\n**Success check:** correct adverb placement in ≥3 sentences. **Queue:** frequency + days recycle in U5 (free time) and U9.\n**Coach notes:** word order is the challenge (not the words) — drill the \"before the main verb\" position; expect \"I get up always\".\n\n---\n\n## S0-U4-L05 — Unit Speaking Task: Describe Your Typical Day (+ Review)\n\n**Metadata:** Stage 0 / A1 · Unit 4 · ~30 min · recorded + live (assessment-style) · prerequisites: L01–L04.\n\n**Objectives (can-do):**\n- The learner can describe their typical day in 6–8 connected sentences (activities + times + frequency).\n- The learner can ask another person about their routine.\n- The learner records a **\"my typical day\"** description (portfolio proof).\n\n**Target language:** integrated Unit-4 language. **Grammar:** present simple (I + he/she) + time + frequency consolidated.\n**Accent Lab focus:** 3rd-person -s + sentence rhythm; each learner's flagged weak sound.\n**Arabic support:** ~45%. **Mindset focus:** you can now narrate a whole day — a big jump from week 1.\n\n**Materials:** a model \"my day\" description; a checklist card; recording prompt; Unit-4 quiz (Google Form).\n\n**Lesson flow:**\n\n| # | Phase | Content | ~Time |\n|--|--|--|--|\n| 1 | Warm-up / recall (retrieval-heavy) | Chain recall: routine verbs, he/she -s, times, frequency. | 5 min |\n| 2 | Input/model | Watch a model \"my typical day\" (7 sentences with times + frequency). | 4 min |\n| 3 | Notice | Break the model into learned frames; build the \"good routine description\" checklist. | 4 min |\n| 4 | Accent Lab clinic | Each learner drills their weakest sound flagged in U4 (often the 3rd-person -s). | 4 min |\n| 5 | Guided output | Rehearse the full day with the card; peer practice (pairs). | 6 min |\n| 6 | Free output (task) | Record the typical-day description (morning → evening, with times + frequency). | 4 min |\n| 7 | Feedback | Self + peer check vs checklist; coach group-level delayed feedback. | 2 min |\n| 8 | Assignment/queue | Post the routine description; complete Unit-4 quiz; celebrate progress. | 1 min |\n\n**Differentiation:** Support — 6 sentences with the card. Stretch — describe your day AND a family member's day (I vs he/she).\n**Success check (Unit-4 summary):** connected 6–8 sentence routine, intelligible, correct present simple (incl. he/she -s) + times (rubric: Fluency + Accuracy + Pronunciation at A1). **Queue:** routine description recycles into Unit 10 review.\n**Coach notes:** ~5 weeks in — this is a strong proof clip (learners now produce a paragraph of connected speech). Log with consent in the Cohort Tracker.\n\n---\n\n## Unit 4 — end-of-unit check (formative)\n\n- **Quiz (Google Form):** present simple (I vs he/she -s); choose the -s sound; telling time; frequency-adverb order.\n- **Speaking task:** the L05 typical-day description, scored on the rubric (A1 threshold).\n- **Feeds:** Cohort Tracker weekly activity + pronunciation notes; updates each learner's weak-sound flag.\n\n---\n\n*Next: Unit 5 — Free Time & Ability (like + -ing, can), continuing build-just-ahead of the cohort.*\n","u5":"# Stage 0 · Unit 5 — Free Time & Ability (full lessons, v1.0)\n\n> **Spec task:** 1.3 · **Built on:** `curriculum/02-lesson-template.md` · **Map:** `stage0/00-stage0-unit-map.md`\n> **Arabic support:** ~45% (fading), instructions/explanations in Egyptian Arabic; learner outputs in English.\n> **Unit goal:** by the end, the learner can talk about hobbies and likes/dislikes, and say what they\n> can and can't do — and can record a short \"my free time\" piece.\n> **Grammar spine:** like/love/hate + -ing · can / can't for ability · Do you like…? / Can you…? ·\n> **Accent Lab:** linking sounds + stressing content words in a sentence (rhythm) · **Mindset:** building\n> a personal English input diet (music/shows/podcasts at your level).\n\n**Unit lessons:** L01 \"I like…\" (likes & dislikes + -ing) · L02 Hobbies & free-time vocab · L03 \"Can you…?\"\n(ability) · L04 Asking questions: \"Do you like…?\" mini-conversations · L05 Unit speaking task: talk about\nyour free time + review.\n\n**Assets to produce:** hobby flashcards, a \"my free time\" clip (American model), like/love/hate scale\nvisual, can/can't audio, linking-and-stress audio, a Google Form per lesson, community voice-note prompts.\n\n---\n\n## S0-U5-L01 — \"I Like…\" — Likes & Dislikes (+ -ing)\n\n**Metadata:** Stage 0 / A1 · Unit 5 · ~30 min · recorded + live · prerequisites: Unit 4.\n\n**Objectives (can-do):**\n- The learner can express likes/dislikes with like/love/hate (+ noun and + verb-ing).\n- The learner can say *\"I like football. I love reading. I don't like cooking.\"*\n- The learner can use *don't like* for dislikes.\n\n**Target language:** like/love/hate; don't like; common activities (reading, cooking, swimming, watching TV).\n**Grammar:** like + noun / like + verb-ing; negative *don't like*. **Accent Lab focus:** -ing endings\n(/ɪŋ/, not /ɪn/ or /ɪŋg/); linking *\"I-like\"*.\n**Arabic support:** ~45%. **Mindset focus:** find English content you *enjoy* — like it and you'll do it.\n\n**Materials:** activity flashcards; like/love/hate scale; clip; frames slide; Google Form.\n\n**Lesson flow:**\n\n| # | Phase | Content | ~Time |\n|--|--|--|--|\n| 1 | Warm-up / recall | Retrieval: 2 frequency sentences from U4 (\"I always…\"). | 4 min |\n| 2 | Input | Clip: \"I love reading. I like football. I don't like cooking.\"; text + Arabic gloss. | 6 min |\n| 3 | Notice | like/love/hate scale; like + -ing; negative *don't like*; explain (Arabic). | 6 min |\n| 4 | Accent Lab | -ing endings (readi**ng**, swimmi**ng**) — clean /ŋ/; link *\"I-like\"*. | 4 min |\n| 5 | Guided output | Say 3 likes + 2 dislikes with -ing (*\"I like watching TV. I don't like running.\"*). | 6 min |\n| 6 | Free output | Live/pairs: tell a partner 2 things you love and 1 you hate; record one. | 3 min |\n| 7 | Feedback | Check like + -ing form + clean -ing sound; queue weak items. | — |\n| 8 | Assignment | Post a voice note: 2 likes + 1 dislike (with -ing). Likes quiz form. | 1 min |\n\n**Differentiation:** Support — like + noun only (\"I like football\"). Stretch — like/love/hate + -ing across 5 activities.\n**Success check:** learner expresses ≥3 likes/dislikes intelligibly. **Queue:** like + -ing recycles in L04 questions and U9 (-ing).\n**Coach notes:** common errors: \"I like to cooking\" or dropping -ing; and /ɪn/ for /ɪŋ/. Target the -ing form and sound gently.\n\n---\n\n## S0-U5-L02 — Hobbies & Free-Time Vocabulary\n\n**Metadata:** Stage 0 / A1 · Unit 5 · ~30 min · recorded + live · prerequisites: L01.\n\n**Objectives (can-do):**\n- The learner can name common hobbies and free-time activities (sports, music, games, going out).\n- The learner can say what they do in their free time (*\"In my free time, I…\"*).\n- The learner can use *play / go / do* with activities (play football, go swimming, do yoga — basic).\n\n**Target language:** hobbies vocab; *play + game/sport*, *go + -ing*, *do + activity*; \"In my free time…\".\n**Grammar:** collocations play/go/do (introductory). **Accent Lab focus:** stress the activity word;\nlinking *\"go-out\"*.\n**Arabic support:** ~45%. **Mindset focus:** pick ONE English show/podcast to start your input diet this week.\n\n**Materials:** hobby flashcards grouped by play/go/do; clip; frames slide; Google Form (match verb → activity).\n\n**Lesson flow:**\n\n| # | Phase | Content | ~Time |\n|--|--|--|--|\n| 1 | Warm-up / recall | Retrieval: your likes from L01. | 4 min |\n| 2 | Input | Clip: \"In my free time, I play football and go swimming.\"; text + gloss. | 6 min |\n| 3 | Notice | Group activities by play/go/do; \"In my free time…\"; explain (Arabic) the collocations simply. | 6 min |\n| 4 | Accent Lab | Stress the content word (*\"play FOOTball\"*); link *\"go-out\"*. | 4 min |\n| 5 | Guided output | Say what you do: *\"I play…, I go…, I do…\"* (3–4 activities). | 6 min |\n| 6 | Free output | Live/pairs: describe your free time in 3 sentences; record. | 3 min |\n| 7 | Feedback | Check play/go/do choice + stress; queue weak items. | — |\n| 8 | Assignment | Post: 3 free-time activities (using play/go/do). Match-verb quiz form. | 1 min |\n\n**Differentiation:** Support — 2 activities with \"I like…\". Stretch — full play/go/do set + frequency (from U4).\n**Success check:** learner names ≥3 hobbies with reasonable collocations. **Queue:** hobbies recycle in L03–L05.\n**Coach notes:** don't over-drill play/go/do rules at A1 — accept and gently model correct collocations; the goal is communication.\n\n---\n\n## S0-U5-L03 — \"Can You…?\" — Ability\n\n**Metadata:** Stage 0 / A1 · Unit 5 · ~30 min · recorded + live · prerequisites: L02.\n\n**Objectives (can-do):**\n- The learner can say what they can and can't do (*\"I can swim. I can't drive.\"*).\n- The learner can ask *\"Can you…?\"* and answer (*\"Yes, I can. / No, I can't.\"*).\n- The learner can talk about others' abilities (*\"She can cook well.\"*).\n\n**Target language:** can/can't + base verb; ability verbs (swim, drive, cook, sing, play the guitar…).\n**Grammar:** can/can't (same for all persons — no -s!); Can you…? short answers. **Accent Lab focus:**\nweak *can* /kən/ vs strong *can't* /kænt/ in natural speech.\n**Arabic support:** ~40%. **Mindset focus:** \"I can't… yet\" — add \"yet\" and keep a growth mindset.\n\n**Materials:** ability flashcards; can/can't audio (weak vs strong); frames slide; Google Form.\n\n**Lesson flow:**\n\n| # | Phase | Content | ~Time |\n|--|--|--|--|\n| 1 | Warm-up / recall | Retrieval: free-time activities from L02. | 4 min |\n| 2 | Input | Clip: \"I can swim but I can't drive. Can you cook?\"; text + gloss. | 6 min |\n| 3 | Notice | can/can't + base verb; **no -s with he/she** (She can cook, not \"cans\"); short answers. | 6 min |\n| 4 | Accent Lab | weak *can* vs strong *can't*; hear the difference (I can /kən/ swim vs I can't /kænt/ swim). | 5 min |\n| 5 | Guided output | Say 3 things you can + 2 you can't; ask a partner *\"Can you…?\"* | 6 min |\n| 6 | Free output | Live/pairs: find one thing you both can do and one neither can; record. | 3 min |\n| 7 | Feedback | Check no-3rd-person-s with can + can/can't distinction; queue. | — |\n| 8 | Assignment | Post: 2 things you can + 1 you can't do. | 1 min |\n\n**Differentiation:** Support — I can / I can't only. Stretch — he/she can + a follow-up question.\n**Success check:** correct can/can't ability statements + question. **Queue:** can recycles in U6 (requests) and U8 (directions help).\n**Coach notes:** two wins here — no -s on modals (\"she can cook\") and hearing weak/strong can. The mindset \"…yet\" reframe is powerful; use it.\n\n---\n\n## S0-U5-L04 — Asking Questions: \"Do You Like…?\" Mini-Conversations\n\n**Metadata:** Stage 0 / A1 · Unit 5 · ~30 min · recorded + live · prerequisites: L03.\n\n**Objectives (can-do):**\n- The learner can ask **Do you like…?** questions and give short answers (*\"Yes, I do. / No, I don't.\"*).\n- The learner can keep a short conversation going (question → answer → follow-up).\n- The learner can ask *\"What do you like doing?\"* (basic).\n\n**Target language:** Do you like + -ing?; short answers; follow-ups (*\"Me too. / Really? Why?\"*).\n**Grammar:** present simple questions with *do*; short answers. **Accent Lab focus:** question intonation\n(rising for yes/no); linking *\"do-you\"* → /djə/.\n**Arabic support:** ~40%. **Mindset focus:** conversation is a skill — a few good questions carry you far.\n\n**Materials:** question prompt cards; a mini-dialogue model; frames slide; Google Form.\n\n**Lesson flow:**\n\n| # | Phase | Content | ~Time |\n|--|--|--|--|\n| 1 | Warm-up / recall | Retrieval: can/can't statements (L03) + a like (L01). | 4 min |\n| 2 | Input | Model mini-dialogue: \"Do you like football?\" \"Yes, I do! Do you?\" \"No, I don't.\"; text + gloss. | 6 min |\n| 3 | Notice | Do you like…? + short answers; keeping it going (\"Me too\", \"Really?\"); explain (Arabic). | 6 min |\n| 4 | Accent Lab | Rising yes/no intonation; natural *\"d'you like…?\"* linking. | 4 min |\n| 5 | Guided output | Card drill: ask/answer *\"Do you like…?\"* in pairs; add one follow-up each. | 6 min |\n| 6 | Free output | Live/pairs: a 4-line conversation (Q → A → follow-up → A); record. | 4 min |\n| 7 | Feedback | Check question form + short answers + intonation. | — |\n| 8 | Assignment | Post: ask 2 classmates *\"Do you like…?\"* in the community and reply to theirs. | — |\n\n**Differentiation:** Support — ask/answer with the card. Stretch — 6-line conversation with 2 follow-ups.\n**Success check:** learner asks a correct Do-you-like question + gives short answers in a mini-exchange. **Queue:** question forms feed U6 (shopping Qs) and U8.\n**Coach notes:** this is the first real back-and-forth conversation — celebrate it. Common error: \"You like…?\" without *do*; and answering \"Yes, I like\" instead of \"Yes, I do\".\n\n---\n\n## S0-U5-L05 — Unit Speaking Task: Talk About Your Free Time (+ Review)\n\n**Metadata:** Stage 0 / A1 · Unit 5 · ~30 min · recorded + live (assessment-style) · prerequisites: L01–L04.\n\n**Objectives (can-do):**\n- The learner can talk about their free time in 6–8 connected sentences (likes, hobbies, abilities).\n- The learner can have a short conversation asking a partner about their free time.\n- The learner records a **\"my free time\"** piece (portfolio proof).\n\n**Target language:** integrated Unit-5 language. **Grammar:** like + -ing, can/can't, do-questions consolidated.\n**Accent Lab focus:** linking + content-word stress + -ing; each learner's flagged weak sound.\n**Arabic support:** ~40%. **Mindset focus:** you can now hold a mini-conversation — that's real progress.\n\n**Materials:** a model \"my free time\" description + mini-dialogue; a checklist card; recording prompt; Unit-5 quiz (Google Form).\n\n**Lesson flow:**\n\n| # | Phase | Content | ~Time |\n|--|--|--|--|\n| 1 | Warm-up / recall (retrieval-heavy) | Chain recall: likes (+ -ing), hobbies (play/go/do), can/can't, Do you like…? | 5 min |\n| 2 | Input/model | Watch a model \"my free time\" description + a short conversation. | 4 min |\n| 3 | Notice | Break into learned frames; build the \"good free-time talk\" checklist. | 4 min |\n| 4 | Accent Lab clinic | Each learner drills their weakest sound flagged in U5 (often -ing or can/can't). | 4 min |\n| 5 | Guided output | Rehearse the description + a Q&A with the card; peer practice (pairs). | 6 min |\n| 6 | Free output (task) | Record: describe your free time (likes + hobbies + abilities) AND ask/answer one question. | 4 min |\n| 7 | Feedback | Self + peer check vs checklist; coach group-level delayed feedback. | 2 min |\n| 8 | Assignment/queue | Post the free-time piece; complete Unit-5 quiz; celebrate progress. | 1 min |\n\n**Differentiation:** Support — 6 sentences with the card. Stretch — full description + a 4-line conversation with a partner.\n**Success check (Unit-5 summary):** connected 6–8 sentence free-time talk + a short exchange, intelligible, correct like + -ing and can/can't (rubric: Fluency + Accuracy + Pronunciation + Interaction at A1). **Queue:** free-time talk recycles into Unit 10 review.\n**Coach notes:** ~6 weeks in — the Interaction dimension now appears (mini-conversations). This clip shows conversational ability emerging; log with consent.\n\n---\n\n## Unit 5 — end-of-unit check (formative)\n\n- **Quiz (Google Form):** like + -ing; play/go/do; can/can't (no -s); Do you like…? question form + short answers.\n- **Speaking task:** the L05 free-time talk + short exchange, scored on the rubric (A1 threshold; Interaction included).\n- **Feeds:** Cohort Tracker weekly activity + pronunciation notes; updates each learner's weak-sound flag.\n\n---\n\n*Next: Unit 6 — Food & Shopping (some/any, would like, prices), continuing build-just-ahead of the cohort.*\n","u6":"# Stage 0 · Unit 6 — Food & Shopping (full lessons, v1.0)\n\n> **Spec task:** 1.3 · **Built on:** `curriculum/02-lesson-template.md` · **Map:** `stage0/00-stage0-unit-map.md`\n> **Arabic support:** ~40% (fading), instructions/explanations in Egyptian Arabic; learner outputs in English.\n> **Unit goal:** by the end, the learner can talk about food, ask prices, and order/request politely —\n> and can perform a café/shop role-play from memory.\n> **Grammar spine:** some/any · would like (\"I'd like…\") · countable vs uncountable (intro) · how much /\n> how many · **Accent Lab:** clear numbers/prices + polite rising intonation for requests · **Mindset:**\n> getting real-world speaking reps (order in English; use it outside class).\n\n**Unit lessons:** L01 Food & drink vocab (I like/eat…) · L02 some/any + countable/uncountable (intro) ·\nL03 \"I'd like…\" (ordering & requests) · L04 Prices & shopping (\"How much is it?\") · L05 Unit speaking task:\nrole-play a café/shop + review.\n\n**Assets to produce:** food/drink flashcards, a menu + shop-price list, a café/shop role-play video\n(American model), price/number audio, polite-intonation audio, a Google Form per lesson, community prompts.\n\n---\n\n## S0-U6-L01 — Food & Drink Vocabulary (I like / I eat…)\n\n**Metadata:** Stage 0 / A1 · Unit 6 · ~30 min · recorded + live · prerequisites: Unit 5.\n\n**Objectives (can-do):**\n- The learner can name common foods and drinks (bread, rice, chicken, fruit, water, tea, coffee, juice…).\n- The learner can say what they eat/drink and like (*\"I eat rice. I drink tea. I like fruit.\"*).\n- The learner can say meals (breakfast, lunch, dinner).\n\n**Target language:** food/drink vocab; meals; *\"I eat/drink/like ___.\"* **Grammar:** present simple recycled\n(eat/drink + like from U5). **Accent Lab focus:** clear vowels in food words (rice, juice, bread); linking.\n**Arabic support:** ~40%. **Mindset focus:** name your food in English while you eat today (real-life reps).\n\n**Materials:** food/drink flashcards; meals visual; clip; frames slide; Google Form (food vs drink).\n\n**Lesson flow:**\n\n| # | Phase | Content | ~Time |\n|--|--|--|--|\n| 1 | Warm-up / recall | Retrieval: 2 likes with -ing from U5 (\"I like cooking\"). | 4 min |\n| 2 | Input | Clip: \"For breakfast I eat bread and drink tea. I like fruit.\"; text + Arabic gloss. | 6 min |\n| 3 | Notice | Food/drink vocab + meals; recycle eat/drink/like; explain (Arabic) any new words. | 6 min |\n| 4 | Accent Lab | Clear vowels: rice /aɪ/, juice /uː/, bread /e/; link *\"I-eat\"*. | 4 min |\n| 5 | Guided output | Say what you eat/drink at each meal (*\"For lunch I eat ___.\"*). | 6 min |\n| 6 | Free output | Live/pairs: tell a partner your favourite food + drink; record one. | 3 min |\n| 7 | Feedback | Check vocab + clear vowels; queue weak items. | — |\n| 8 | Assignment | Post a voice note: what you eat/drink for breakfast. Food/drink quiz form. | 1 min |\n\n**Differentiation:** Support — 4 food words + \"I like…\". Stretch — a full meal description with 3 meals.\n**Success check:** learner names ≥5 foods/drinks and forms eat/drink/like sentences. **Queue:** food words feed L02–L05.\n**Coach notes:** keep it personal and appetising — food is a high-motivation topic; learners talk easily here.\n\n---\n\n## S0-U6-L02 — some / any + Countable vs Uncountable (Intro)\n\n**Metadata:** Stage 0 / A1 · Unit 6 · ~30 min · recorded + live · prerequisites: L01.\n\n**Objectives (can-do):**\n- The learner can use **some** (positive) and **any** (questions/negatives) with food.\n- The learner can distinguish countable (an apple, two eggs) vs uncountable (rice, water) at a basic level.\n- The learner can say what they have/haven't got in the kitchen (*\"I have some bread. I don't have any milk.\"*).\n\n**Target language:** some/any; countable/uncountable food; have got recycled (U2). **Grammar:** some (+),\nany (−/?); a/an with countables. **Accent Lab focus:** weak *some* /səm/; linking *\"some-apples\"*.\n**Arabic support:** ~40%. **Mindset focus:** small consistent effort — like stocking a kitchen a bit each day.\n\n**Materials:** countable/uncountable sort visual; fridge/kitchen image; frames slide; Google Form (some/any; count/uncount).\n\n**Lesson flow:**\n\n| # | Phase | Content | ~Time |\n|--|--|--|--|\n| 1 | Warm-up / recall | Retrieval: food words from L01. | 4 min |\n| 2 | Input | Clip: \"I have some rice but I don't have any eggs. Is there any milk?\"; text + gloss. | 6 min |\n| 3 | Notice | some (+) / any (−,?); countable (eggs, apples) vs uncountable (rice, water, milk); explain (Arabic). | 7 min |\n| 4 | Accent Lab | Weak *some* /səm/; link *\"some-bread\"*. | 4 min |\n| 5 | Guided output | Look at the fridge image: *\"There is some ___. There isn't any ___.\"* | 6 min |\n| 6 | Free output | Live/pairs: say 2 things you have and 1 you don't (some/any); record. | 3 min |\n| 7 | Feedback | Check some/any use; queue weak items. | — |\n| 8 | Assignment | Post: 2 foods you have (some) + 1 you don't (any). | — |\n\n**Differentiation:** Support — some in positives only. Stretch — full some/any across +/−/? with count vs uncount.\n**Success check:** correct some/any in ≥3 statements. **Queue:** some/any feed L03–L04 (ordering, quantities).\n**Coach notes:** keep countable/uncountable light at A1 — a few clear examples, not exhaustive rules. Communication first.\n\n---\n\n## S0-U6-L03 — \"I'd Like…\" — Ordering & Requests\n\n**Metadata:** Stage 0 / A1 · Unit 6 · ~30 min · recorded + live · prerequisites: L02.\n\n**Objectives (can-do):**\n- The learner can order food/drink politely using **I'd like** (*\"I'd like a coffee, please.\"*).\n- The learner can make polite requests (*\"Can I have…?\"*) and respond (*\"Here you are.\" \"Thank you.\"*).\n- The learner can use *would you like…?* to offer (basic).\n\n**Target language:** I'd like / Can I have…? / Would you like…?; please/thank you (recycled U0).\n**Grammar:** would like (polite want); Can I have…? **Accent Lab focus:** polite **rising intonation** on\nrequests; clear /aɪd/ in \"I'd\".\n**Arabic support:** ~35%. **Mindset focus:** politeness + a smile makes real-world English easier — try it out.\n\n**Materials:** café menu; role-play model clip; frames slide; Google Form.\n\n**Lesson flow:**\n\n| # | Phase | Content | ~Time |\n|--|--|--|--|\n| 1 | Warm-up / recall | Retrieval: some/any with food (L02). | 4 min |\n| 2 | Input | Café clip: \"Can I help you?\" \"I'd like a tea, please.\" \"Here you are.\"; text + gloss. | 6 min |\n| 3 | Notice | I'd like (polite) vs I want; Can I have…?; Would you like…?; explain (Arabic) the politeness. | 6 min |\n| 4 | Accent Lab | Rising polite intonation; clear \"I'd like\"; soft \"please\". | 4 min |\n| 5 | Guided output | Order from the menu: *\"I'd like ___, please.\"*; partner responds. | 6 min |\n| 6 | Free output | Live/pairs: order 2 items politely from the menu; swap roles; record. | 4 min |\n| 7 | Feedback | Check I'd like form + polite intonation. | — |\n| 8 | Assignment | Post: order your favourite café items (*\"I'd like…\"*). | — |\n\n**Differentiation:** Support — one item with \"I'd like…, please.\" Stretch — full mini-order with a follow-up (size, \"anything else?\").\n**Success check:** learner places a polite order with correct form + intonation. **Queue:** requests recycle in L04–L05 and U8 (asking for help).\n**Coach notes:** learners often default to \"I want\" (can sound rude) — reframe \"I'd like\" as the polite, natural choice. Model warmth.\n\n---\n\n## S0-U6-L04 — Prices & Shopping: \"How Much Is It?\"\n\n**Metadata:** Stage 0 / A1 · Unit 6 · ~30 min · recorded + live · prerequisites: L03.\n\n**Objectives (can-do):**\n- The learner can ask and understand prices (*\"How much is it?\" / \"It's ___.\"*).\n- The learner can use **how much** (uncountable/price) and **how many** (countable) at a basic level.\n- The learner can complete a simple shopping exchange.\n\n**Target language:** how much / how many; prices/money; shop language (*\"That's ___, please.\"*).\n**Grammar:** how much (price/uncount) vs how many (count). **Accent Lab focus:** clear numbers/prices\n(recycle 20–100 from U2); the -teen/-ty distinction in prices.\n**Arabic support:** ~35%. **Mindset focus:** you're now handling real transactions — huge practical win.\n\n**Materials:** shop price list; number/price audio; frames slide; Google Form (listen → write the price).\n\n**Lesson flow:**\n\n| # | Phase | Content | ~Time |\n|--|--|--|--|\n| 1 | Warm-up / recall | Retrieval: order something politely (L03) + numbers 20–100 (U2). | 4 min |\n| 2 | Input | Shop clip: \"How much is this?\" \"It's fifteen.\" \"How many do you want?\"; text + gloss. | 6 min |\n| 3 | Notice | how much (price/uncount) vs how many (count); saying prices; explain (Arabic). | 6 min |\n| 4 | Accent Lab | Clear prices; thir**teen** vs **thir**ty in a price (13 vs 30 matters!). | 5 min |\n| 5 | Guided output | Ask prices from the list: *\"How much is the ___?\" \"It's ___.\"* | 6 min |\n| 6 | Free output | Live/pairs: a shopping exchange — ask 2 prices + buy; record. | 3 min |\n| 7 | Feedback | Check how much/how many + clear prices; queue. | — |\n| 8 | Assignment | Post: ask the price of 2 things and answer. Price quiz form. | 1 min |\n\n**Differentiation:** Support — how much + say a price. Stretch — full exchange with how much + how many + total.\n**Success check:** learner asks/answers ≥2 prices intelligibly. **Queue:** numbers/prices consolidate; feed U10 review.\n**Coach notes:** the -teen/-ty price confusion has real consequences — reinforce the stress cue from U2. Practice realistic prices.\n\n---\n\n## S0-U6-L05 — Unit Speaking Task: Role-Play a Café/Shop (+ Review)\n\n**Metadata:** Stage 0 / A1 · Unit 6 · ~30 min · recorded + live (assessment-style) · prerequisites: L01–L04.\n\n**Objectives (can-do):**\n- The learner can perform a café or shop role-play (greet → order/ask price → pay → thank).\n- The learner can play both customer and server.\n- The learner records a **café/shop role-play** (interactive proof).\n\n**Target language:** integrated Unit-6 language. **Grammar:** I'd like + some/any + how much consolidated.\n**Accent Lab focus:** polite intonation + clear prices; each learner's flagged weak sound.\n**Arabic support:** ~35%. **Mindset focus:** this is English you'll actually use — notice how usable it feels.\n\n**Materials:** café + shop menus/price lists; a model role-play; a role-play checklist card; recording prompt; Unit-6 quiz (Google Form).\n\n**Lesson flow:**\n\n| # | Phase | Content | ~Time |\n|--|--|--|--|\n| 1 | Warm-up / recall (retrieval-heavy) | Chain recall: food words, some/any, I'd like, how much. | 5 min |\n| 2 | Input/model | Watch a model café role-play (greet → order → price → thanks). | 4 min |\n| 3 | Notice | Break the role-play into stages; build the \"good service exchange\" checklist. | 4 min |\n| 4 | Accent Lab clinic | Each learner drills their weakest sound flagged in U6 (often prices or intonation). | 4 min |\n| 5 | Guided output | Rehearse the role-play both roles with the card; peer practice (pairs). | 6 min |\n| 6 | Free output (task) | Record the role-play (in pairs live, or solo playing both parts). | 4 min |\n| 7 | Feedback | Self + peer check vs checklist; coach group-level delayed feedback. | 2 min |\n| 8 | Assignment/queue | Post the role-play; complete Unit-6 quiz; celebrate progress. | 1 min |\n\n**Differentiation:** Support — customer role only, short order. Stretch — both roles + handle \"anything else?\" and total.\n**Success check (Unit-6 summary):** completes a café/shop exchange with polite requests + prices, intelligible (rubric: Fluency + Accuracy + Pronunciation + Interaction at A1). **Queue:** role-play recycles into Unit 10 review.\n**Coach notes:** ~7 weeks in — a functional real-world role-play is a compelling proof clip and very motivating. Log with consent.\n\n---\n\n## Unit 6 — end-of-unit check (formative)\n\n- **Quiz (Google Form):** some/any; countable vs uncountable; I'd like form; how much vs how many; prices.\n- **Speaking task:** the L05 café/shop role-play, scored on the rubric (A1 threshold; Interaction included).\n- **Feeds:** Cohort Tracker weekly activity + pronunciation notes; updates each learner's weak-sound flag.\n\n---\n\n*Next: Unit 7 — Describing People & Things (adjectives, have got), continuing build-just-ahead of the cohort.*\n","u7":"# Stage 0 · Unit 7 — Describing People & Things (full lessons, v1.0)\n\n> **Spec task:** 1.3 · **Built on:** `curriculum/02-lesson-template.md` · **Map:** `stage0/00-stage0-unit-map.md`\n> **Arabic support:** ~35% (fading), instructions/explanations in Egyptian Arabic; learner outputs in English.\n> **Unit goal:** by the end, the learner can describe things and people using adjectives and *have got* —\n> and can record a description of a person they know.\n> **Grammar spine:** adjective + noun order · colours · *have got* (for features/possessions) ·\n> **Accent Lab:** adjective stress + the /w/ and /r/ sounds · **Mindset:** progress mindset — noticing\n> and celebrating small wins.\n\n**Unit lessons:** L01 Colours & basic adjectives · L02 Describing things (adjective + noun) ·\nL03 Describing people's appearance · L04 have got (\"I've got brown eyes\") · L05 Unit speaking task:\ndescribe a person you know + review.\n\n**Assets to produce:** colour + adjective flashcards, object/person images, a \"describe someone\" clip\n(American model), /w/ and /r/ minimal audio, have got frames, a Google Form per lesson, community prompts.\n\n---\n\n## S0-U7-L01 — Colours & Basic Adjectives\n\n**Metadata:** Stage 0 / A1 · Unit 7 · ~30 min · recorded + live · prerequisites: Unit 6.\n\n**Objectives (can-do):**\n- The learner can name colours and basic adjectives (big/small, old/new, good/bad, nice, beautiful, tall/short).\n- The learner can use *\"It's + adjective\"* (*\"It's red. It's big.\"*).\n- The learner can ask *\"What colour is it?\"*\n\n**Target language:** colours; basic opposite adjectives; *\"It's ___.\" / \"What colour is it?\"*\n**Grammar:** adjective after *to be*. **Accent Lab focus:** /r/ in *red/brown/orange*; /w/ in *white/yellow*.\n**Arabic support:** ~35%. **Mindset focus:** notice a small win from last week — name it out loud.\n\n**Materials:** colour + adjective flashcards; objects to describe; frames slide; Google Form.\n\n**Lesson flow:**\n\n| # | Phase | Content | ~Time |\n|--|--|--|--|\n| 1 | Warm-up / recall | Retrieval: order something politely (U6) + one object (U3). | 4 min |\n| 2 | Input | Clip: \"It's a red car. It's big and new.\"; text + Arabic gloss. | 6 min |\n| 3 | Notice | Colours + opposite adjectives; *\"It's + adjective\"*; *\"What colour is it?\"* | 6 min |\n| 4 | Accent Lab | /r/ (red, brown) vs /w/ (white, yellow) — lip rounding for /w/, no roll for /r/. | 5 min |\n| 5 | Guided output | Describe flashcards: *\"It's + colour + adjective.\"*; ask colours. | 6 min |\n| 6 | Free output | Live/pairs: describe 3 objects around you (colour + adjective); record one. | 3 min |\n| 7 | Feedback | Check adjective use + /r/,/w/; queue weak items. | — |\n| 8 | Assignment | Post a voice note: describe 3 things by colour + one adjective. Colour/adjective quiz form. | 1 min |\n\n**Differentiation:** Support — colour only (\"It's blue\"). Stretch — colour + 2 adjectives.\n**Success check:** learner uses ≥3 colours/adjectives intelligibly. **Queue:** colours/adjectives feed L02–L05.\n**Coach notes:** /r/ vs /w/ can blur for Arabic speakers on some words — the rounding cue for /w/ helps. Keep it light and visual.\n\n---\n\n## S0-U7-L02 — Describing Things (Adjective + Noun)\n\n**Metadata:** Stage 0 / A1 · Unit 7 · ~30 min · recorded + live · prerequisites: L01.\n\n**Objectives (can-do):**\n- The learner can put adjectives **before** the noun (*\"a big house\", \"a beautiful car\"*) — key word-order fix.\n- The learner can describe objects with 2 adjectives (*\"a small old phone\"*).\n- The learner can compare simply (*\"This one is better/nicer.\"* — basic, optional).\n\n**Target language:** adjective + noun phrases; a/an + adjective + noun. **Grammar:** adjective **before** noun\n(English order, unlike Arabic). **Accent Lab focus:** stress the noun; smooth adjective+noun linking.\n**Arabic support:** ~35%. **Mindset focus:** progress is built one small correct sentence at a time.\n\n**Materials:** object images; adjective+noun cards; frames slide; Google Form (word-order fix).\n\n**Lesson flow:**\n\n| # | Phase | Content | ~Time |\n|--|--|--|--|\n| 1 | Warm-up / recall | Retrieval: colours/adjectives from L01. | 4 min |\n| 2 | Input | Clip: \"It's a beautiful old house. I have a new black bag.\"; text + gloss. | 6 min |\n| 3 | Notice | **Adjective goes BEFORE the noun** in English (contrast with Arabic order); explain (Arabic) clearly. | 7 min |\n| 4 | Accent Lab | Stress the noun (*\"a big HOUSE\"*); link adjective+noun smoothly. | 4 min |\n| 5 | Guided output | Build phrases from images: *\"a + adjective + noun\"*; then 2 adjectives. | 6 min |\n| 6 | Free output | Live/pairs: describe 3 of your things with adjective + noun; record. | 3 min |\n| 7 | Feedback | Target adjective–noun order (key error) + noun stress. | — |\n| 8 | Assignment | Post: describe 3 things using adjective + noun. | — |\n\n**Differentiation:** Support — one adjective + noun. Stretch — 2 adjectives + a simple comparison.\n**Success check:** correct adjective–noun order in ≥3 phrases. **Queue:** adjective+noun recycles in L03–L05.\n**Coach notes:** Arabic places the adjective *after* the noun, so expect \"a house big\". This is a signature word-order fix — drill it explicitly and warmly; it recurs at every level.\n\n---\n\n## S0-U7-L03 — Describing People's Appearance\n\n**Metadata:** Stage 0 / A1 · Unit 7 · ~30 min · recorded + live · prerequisites: L02.\n\n**Objectives (can-do):**\n- The learner can describe appearance (tall/short, young/old, hair and eye words).\n- The learner can use *He/She is + adjective* and describe height/build.\n- The learner can ask *\"What does he/she look like?\"* (basic).\n\n**Target language:** appearance adjectives; hair (long/short/black/blonde), height/build; *\"He/She is ___.\"*\n**Grammar:** to be + adjective for people; recycle he/she. **Accent Lab focus:** stress in descriptive\nsentences; clear /r/ in *short/hair*.\n**Arabic support:** ~30%. **Mindset focus:** celebrate: you can now paint a picture of a person in English.\n\n**Materials:** people images (varied); appearance flashcards; frames slide; Google Form.\n\n**Lesson flow:**\n\n| # | Phase | Content | ~Time |\n|--|--|--|--|\n| 1 | Warm-up / recall | Retrieval: adjective + noun phrases (L02). | 4 min |\n| 2 | Input | Clip: \"She is tall. She has long black hair.\"; text + gloss. | 6 min |\n| 3 | Notice | Appearance adjectives; *He/She is + adjective*; preview *has/have got hair* (→ L04). | 6 min |\n| 4 | Accent Lab | Sentence stress on key describing words; /r/ in *short/hair*. | 4 min |\n| 5 | Guided output | Describe people in images: height + one feature. | 6 min |\n| 6 | Free output | Live/pairs: describe a famous person or a friend (2–3 sentences); record. | 4 min |\n| 7 | Feedback | Check to-be + adjective + he/she; queue. | — |\n| 8 | Assignment | Post: describe a person in 2–3 sentences (appearance). | — |\n\n**Differentiation:** Support — height + one adjective. Stretch — 4-sentence portrait (height, build, hair, eyes).\n**Success check:** learner describes a person in ≥2 sentences intelligibly. **Queue:** appearance feeds L04 (have got) + L05.\n**Coach notes:** keep descriptions respectful and neutral. Model varied, positive descriptions.\n\n---\n\n## S0-U7-L04 — have got: \"I've Got Brown Eyes\"\n\n**Metadata:** Stage 0 / A1 · Unit 7 · ~30 min · recorded + live · prerequisites: L03.\n\n**Objectives (can-do):**\n- The learner can use **have got / has got** for features and possessions (*\"I've got brown eyes. She's got a car.\"*).\n- The learner can use contractions (I've got / she's got).\n- The learner can ask *\"Have you got…?\"* and answer (*\"Yes, I have. / No, I haven't.\"*).\n\n**Target language:** have got/has got + features/possessions; Have you got…? **Grammar:** have got\n(= have) — contractions + questions. **Accent Lab focus:** contractions *I've /aɪv/, she's /ʃiːz/*; linking.\n**Arabic support:** ~30%. **Mindset focus:** two ways to say \"have\" — you're gaining flexibility.\n\n**Materials:** features flashcards; have got frames; contraction audio; Google Form.\n\n**Lesson flow:**\n\n| # | Phase | Content | ~Time |\n|--|--|--|--|\n| 1 | Warm-up / recall | Retrieval: describe a person's appearance (L03) + have/has from U2. | 4 min |\n| 2 | Input | Clip: \"I've got green eyes. He's got short hair. Have you got a pet?\"; text + gloss. | 6 min |\n| 3 | Notice | have got/has got (= have/has); contractions; questions Have you got…?; explain (Arabic) it equals have. | 6 min |\n| 4 | Accent Lab | Contractions: *I've got, she's got*; don't drop the /v/ /z/; link \"got a\". | 5 min |\n| 5 | Guided output | Describe features: *\"I've got ___. My friend has got ___.\"* | 6 min |\n| 6 | Free output | Live/pairs: ask *\"Have you got…?\"* (a car/pet/brother); answer; record. | 3 min |\n| 7 | Feedback | Check have got/has got + contractions; queue. | — |\n| 8 | Assignment | Post: describe 2 of your features with have got. | — |\n\n**Differentiation:** Support — \"I've got…\" only. Stretch — mix I've/he's got + a question.\n**Success check:** correct have got/has got in ≥3 statements. **Queue:** have got recycles into U10 review.\n**Coach notes:** link back to U2 have/has — reassure learners that \"have\" and \"have got\" mean the same for possession; the contraction /v/ often gets dropped, so drill it.\n\n---\n\n## S0-U7-L05 — Unit Speaking Task: Describe a Person You Know (+ Review)\n\n**Metadata:** Stage 0 / A1 · Unit 7 · ~30 min · recorded + live (assessment-style) · prerequisites: L01–L04.\n\n**Objectives (can-do):**\n- The learner can describe a person they know in 6–8 connected sentences (appearance + features + a like).\n- The learner can ask another person to describe someone.\n- The learner records a **\"describe someone I know\"** piece (portfolio proof).\n\n**Target language:** integrated Unit-7 language. **Grammar:** adjective+noun order, to be + adjective, have got consolidated.\n**Accent Lab focus:** adjective–noun order + /r/,/w/ + contractions; each learner's flagged weak sound.\n**Arabic support:** ~30%. **Mindset focus:** notice your growth — a full portrait in English, from zero 8 weeks ago.\n\n**Materials:** a model person-description; a checklist card; recording prompt; Unit-7 quiz (Google Form).\n\n**Lesson flow:**\n\n| # | Phase | Content | ~Time |\n|--|--|--|--|\n| 1 | Warm-up / recall (retrieval-heavy) | Chain recall: colours/adjectives, adjective+noun order, appearance, have got. | 5 min |\n| 2 | Input/model | Watch a model description of a person (6–7 sentences). | 4 min |\n| 3 | Notice | Break into learned frames; build the \"good person description\" checklist. | 4 min |\n| 4 | Accent Lab clinic | Each learner drills their weakest sound flagged in U7 (often /r/,/w/ or adjective order). | 4 min |\n| 5 | Guided output | Rehearse the full description with the card; peer practice (pairs). | 6 min |\n| 6 | Free output (task) | Record: describe a person you know (appearance + features + one thing they like). | 4 min |\n| 7 | Feedback | Self + peer check vs checklist; coach group-level delayed feedback. | 2 min |\n| 8 | Assignment/queue | Post the description; complete Unit-7 quiz; celebrate progress. | 1 min |\n\n**Differentiation:** Support — 6 sentences with the card. Stretch — describe two people and compare them simply.\n**Success check (Unit-7 summary):** connected 6–8 sentence person description, intelligible, correct adjective order + have got (rubric: Fluency + Accuracy + Pronunciation at A1). **Queue:** person description recycles into Unit 10 review.\n**Coach notes:** ~8 weeks in — descriptive fluency is a clear, visible gain. This clip pairs well with the Day-1 sample; log with consent.\n\n---\n\n## Unit 7 — end-of-unit check (formative)\n\n- **Quiz (Google Form):** colours/adjectives; adjective–noun order; appearance vocab; have got/has got + questions.\n- **Speaking task:** the L05 person description, scored on the rubric (A1 threshold).\n- **Feeds:** Cohort Tracker weekly activity + pronunciation notes; updates each learner's weak-sound flag.\n\n---\n\n*Next: Unit 8 — Places & Directions (imperatives, Is there…?, prepositions of movement), continuing build-just-ahead.*\n","u8":"# Stage 0 · Unit 8 — Places & Directions (full lessons, v1.0)\n\n> **Spec task:** 1.3 · **Built on:** `curriculum/02-lesson-template.md` · **Map:** `stage0/00-stage0-unit-map.md`\n> **Arabic support:** ~30% (fading), instructions/explanations in Egyptian Arabic; learner outputs in English.\n> **Unit goal:** by the end, the learner can ask for and give simple directions and talk about places in\n> a town — and can give directions to a place from memory.\n> **Grammar spine:** places in town · Is there a…? (recycled there is/are) · imperatives (go/turn/take) ·\n> prepositions of movement (to/into/across/along/past) · **Accent Lab:** intonation of instructions +\n> clear vowels in direction words · **Mindset:** confidence in real interactions (ask strangers; be ok with mistakes).\n\n**Unit lessons:** L01 Places in town · L02 \"Is there a…near here?\" · L03 Giving directions (imperatives) ·\nL04 Following & giving a route (prepositions of movement) · L05 Unit speaking task: give directions + review.\n\n**Assets to produce:** places-in-town flashcards, a simple town map, a \"directions\" clip (American model),\ndirection-word audio, imperative + preposition frames, a Google Form per lesson, community prompts.\n\n---\n\n## S0-U8-L01 — Places in Town\n\n**Metadata:** Stage 0 / A1 · Unit 8 · ~30 min · recorded + live · prerequisites: Unit 7.\n\n**Objectives (can-do):**\n- The learner can name common places in a town (bank, supermarket, hospital, school, restaurant, station, pharmacy, mosque/church).\n- The learner can say what places are near them (*\"There is a bank near my house.\"*).\n- The learner can say where they go (*\"I go to the supermarket.\"*).\n\n**Target language:** places in town; *\"There is a ___ near ___.\" / \"I go to the ___.\"*\n**Grammar:** there is + place (recycled U3); go to + place. **Accent Lab focus:** clear vowels in\n*bank, park, station*; stress the place word.\n**Arabic support:** ~30%. **Mindset focus:** confidence grows by doing — plan one real English interaction this week.\n\n**Materials:** places flashcards; town map; clip; frames slide; Google Form (match place → picture).\n\n**Lesson flow:**\n\n| # | Phase | Content | ~Time |\n|--|--|--|--|\n| 1 | Warm-up / recall | Retrieval: describe a person (U7) + there is/are (U3). | 4 min |\n| 2 | Input | Clip: \"There is a bank near my house. I go to the supermarket on Friday.\"; text + Arabic gloss. | 6 min |\n| 3 | Notice | Places vocab; *there is a + place*; *go to the + place*; explain (Arabic) any new words. | 6 min |\n| 4 | Accent Lab | Clear vowels: bank /æ/, park /ɑːr/, station /eɪ/; stress the place. | 4 min |\n| 5 | Guided output | Point on the map: *\"There is a ___.\"*; say 3 places you go to. | 6 min |\n| 6 | Free output | Live/pairs: name 3 places near you; record one. | 3 min |\n| 7 | Feedback | Check vocab + there is/go to; queue weak items. | — |\n| 8 | Assignment | Post a voice note: 3 places near your home. Places quiz form. | 1 min |\n\n**Differentiation:** Support — 4 places + \"There is a…\". Stretch — add frequency (from U4): \"I go to the gym twice a week\".\n**Success check:** learner names ≥4 places intelligibly. **Queue:** places feed L02–L05.\n**Coach notes:** recycle there is/are and go to explicitly — this unit reinforces earlier grammar in a new context (spaced review in action).\n\n---\n\n## S0-U8-L02 — \"Is There a … Near Here?\"\n\n**Metadata:** Stage 0 / A1 · Unit 8 · ~30 min · recorded + live · prerequisites: L01.\n\n**Objectives (can-do):**\n- The learner can ask if a place exists nearby (*\"Is there a pharmacy near here?\"*).\n- The learner can answer (*\"Yes, there is. / No, there isn't.\"*) and say roughly where (*\"It's near the bank.\"*).\n- The learner can use *Excuse me* to start (recycled U0).\n\n**Target language:** Is there a…? + near here; *Excuse me*; location answers (near/next to — recycled U3).\n**Grammar:** Is there…? short answers; prepositions of place recycled. **Accent Lab focus:** polite\n*\"Excuse me\"* + rising question intonation.\n**Arabic support:** ~30%. **Mindset focus:** it's okay to ask strangers — everyone does; a question is not a failure.\n\n**Materials:** town map; a \"asking on the street\" clip; frames slide; Google Form.\n\n**Lesson flow:**\n\n| # | Phase | Content | ~Time |\n|--|--|--|--|\n| 1 | Warm-up / recall | Retrieval: places from L01. | 4 min |\n| 2 | Input | Clip: \"Excuse me, is there a bank near here?\" \"Yes, it's next to the pharmacy.\"; text + gloss. | 6 min |\n| 3 | Notice | Is there a…near here?; short answers; *Excuse me*; location answers; explain (Arabic). | 6 min |\n| 4 | Accent Lab | Polite \"Excuse me\"; rising intonation on the question. | 4 min |\n| 5 | Guided output | Map drill: ask *\"Is there a ___ near here?\"*; partner answers with a location. | 6 min |\n| 6 | Free output | Live/pairs: ask about 2 real places near you; answer; record. | 4 min |\n| 7 | Feedback | Check question form + short answers; queue. | — |\n| 8 | Assignment | Post: ask *\"Is there a ___ near here?\"* to a classmate and answer theirs. | — |\n\n**Differentiation:** Support — the question + yes/no answer. Stretch — add the location (\"It's next to…\").\n**Success check:** learner asks a correct Is-there question + answers. **Queue:** feeds L03 (directions).\n**Coach notes:** connects U0 survival phrases + U3 prepositions + U8 places — a nice integrative moment. Reassure the \"asking is normal\" mindset.\n\n---\n\n## S0-U8-L03 — Giving Directions (Imperatives)\n\n**Metadata:** Stage 0 / A1 · Unit 8 · ~30 min · recorded + live · prerequisites: L02.\n\n**Objectives (can-do):**\n- The learner can give simple directions using imperatives (go straight, turn left/right, take the first/second…).\n- The learner can understand directions given to them.\n- The learner can say *\"It's on the left/right.\"*\n\n**Target language:** imperatives (go, turn, take, stop); left/right/straight; first/second; on the left/right.\n**Grammar:** imperative form (base verb, no subject). **Accent Lab focus:** falling **instruction\nintonation**; clear /l/ vs /r/ in left/right.\n**Arabic support:** ~25%. **Mindset focus:** mistakes when asking directions cost nothing — practice boldly.\n\n**Materials:** town map with a route; direction gestures visual; clip; frames slide; Google Form.\n\n**Lesson flow:**\n\n| # | Phase | Content | ~Time |\n|--|--|--|--|\n| 1 | Warm-up / recall | Retrieval: \"Is there a ___ near here?\" (L02). | 4 min |\n| 2 | Input | Clip giving directions: \"Go straight, turn left, it's on the right.\"; text + gloss. | 6 min |\n| 3 | Notice | Imperatives (no subject); left/right/straight; take the first/second; explain (Arabic). | 6 min |\n| 4 | Accent Lab | /l/ (left) vs /r/ (right) — a key clarity pair; firm falling instruction tone. | 5 min |\n| 5 | Guided output | Follow a route on the map as the teacher gives directions; then give a short route. | 6 min |\n| 6 | Free output | Live/pairs: give directions from A to B on the map; record. | 3 min |\n| 7 | Feedback | Check imperative use + left/right clarity; queue. | — |\n| 8 | Assignment | Post: give directions from your home to a nearby place (2–3 steps). | — |\n\n**Differentiation:** Support — 2 steps (go straight, turn left). Stretch — a 4-step route + \"it's on the…\".\n**Success check:** learner gives a 2–3 step route intelligibly. **Queue:** directions feed L04–L05.\n**Coach notes:** left/right clarity matters practically; drill /l/–/r/. Imperatives are simple (no subject) — a confidence-builder.\n\n---\n\n## S0-U8-L04 — Following & Giving a Route (Prepositions of Movement)\n\n**Metadata:** Stage 0 / A1 · Unit 8 · ~30 min · recorded + live · prerequisites: L03.\n\n**Objectives (can-do):**\n- The learner can use prepositions of movement (go to, into, across, along, past, up/down).\n- The learner can give a fuller route combining imperatives + movement prepositions.\n- The learner can check understanding (*\"Sorry, turn left or right?\"* — recycled survival phrases).\n\n**Target language:** to/into/across/along/past/up/down; combine with imperatives.\n**Grammar:** preposition of movement + place. **Accent Lab focus:** linking *\"go-past-the\"*; clear vowels.\n**Arabic support:** ~25%. **Mindset focus:** you can now handle a real navigation conversation — a big life skill.\n\n**Materials:** detailed town map; movement-preposition visual; frames slide; Google Form.\n\n**Lesson flow:**\n\n| # | Phase | Content | ~Time |\n|--|--|--|--|\n| 1 | Warm-up / recall | Retrieval: give a 2-step direction (L03). | 4 min |\n| 2 | Input | Clip: \"Go along this street, go past the bank, and turn into King Street.\"; text + gloss. | 6 min |\n| 3 | Notice | Movement prepositions (along/past/into/across); combine with imperatives; explain (Arabic). | 6 min |\n| 4 | Accent Lab | Link *\"go-past-the\"*, *\"go-along\"*; keep it smooth. | 4 min |\n| 5 | Guided output | Give a fuller route on the map (3–4 steps with movement prepositions). | 6 min |\n| 6 | Free output | Live/pairs: A asks for a place, B gives a full route; A confirms; record. | 4 min |\n| 7 | Feedback | Check preposition + imperative combo; queue. | — |\n| 8 | Assignment | Post: give a 3–4 step route to a place near you. | — |\n\n**Differentiation:** Support — imperatives + \"to\". Stretch — full route with 3 movement prepositions + a clarification exchange.\n**Success check:** learner gives a 3–4 step route intelligibly. **Queue:** routes recycle into U10 review.\n**Coach notes:** encourage learners to use survival phrases (U0) to clarify — this integrates conversation-management with directions.\n\n---\n\n## S0-U8-L05 — Unit Speaking Task: Give Directions (+ Review)\n\n**Metadata:** Stage 0 / A1 · Unit 8 · ~30 min · recorded + live (assessment-style) · prerequisites: L01–L04.\n\n**Objectives (can-do):**\n- The learner can conduct a full directions exchange (ask → give a multi-step route → confirm/thank).\n- The learner can play both the asker and the direction-giver.\n- The learner records a **directions exchange** (interactive proof).\n\n**Target language:** integrated Unit-8 language. **Grammar:** Is there…? + imperatives + movement prepositions consolidated.\n**Accent Lab focus:** /l/–/r/ + instruction intonation + linking; each learner's flagged weak sound.\n**Arabic support:** ~25%. **Mindset focus:** this is confident, real-world English — celebrate the boldness.\n\n**Materials:** a town map; a model directions dialogue; a checklist card; recording prompt; Unit-8 quiz (Google Form).\n\n**Lesson flow:**\n\n| # | Phase | Content | ~Time |\n|--|--|--|--|\n| 1 | Warm-up / recall (retrieval-heavy) | Chain recall: places, Is there…?, imperatives, movement prepositions. | 5 min |\n| 2 | Input/model | Watch a model directions exchange (ask → route → confirm → thanks). | 4 min |\n| 3 | Notice | Break into stages; build the \"good directions exchange\" checklist. | 4 min |\n| 4 | Accent Lab clinic | Each learner drills their weakest sound flagged in U8 (often /l/–/r/). | 4 min |\n| 5 | Guided output | Rehearse both roles with the map + card; peer practice (pairs). | 6 min |\n| 6 | Free output (task) | Record the directions exchange (in pairs live, or solo playing both parts). | 4 min |\n| 7 | Feedback | Self + peer check vs checklist; coach group-level delayed feedback. | 2 min |\n| 8 | Assignment/queue | Post the exchange; complete Unit-8 quiz; celebrate progress. | 1 min |\n\n**Differentiation:** Support — asker role + a 2-step route. Stretch — both roles + a clarification + a 4-step route.\n**Success check (Unit-8 summary):** completes a directions exchange with a multi-step route, intelligible (rubric: Fluency + Accuracy + Pronunciation + Interaction at A1). **Queue:** directions recycle into Unit 10 review.\n**Coach notes:** ~9 weeks in — a real navigation conversation is a very practical, confidence-boosting proof clip. Log with consent.\n\n---\n\n## Unit 8 — end-of-unit check (formative)\n\n- **Quiz (Google Form):** places vocab; Is there…? form; imperatives; movement prepositions; left/right.\n- **Speaking task:** the L05 directions exchange, scored on the rubric (A1 threshold; Interaction included).\n- **Feeds:** Cohort Tracker weekly activity + pronunciation notes; updates each learner's weak-sound flag.\n\n---\n\n*Next: Unit 9 — Happening Now (present continuous), the last content unit before consolidation (Unit 10).*\n","u9":"# Stage 0 · Unit 9 — Happening Now (full lessons, v1.0)\n\n> **Spec task:** 1.3 · **Built on:** `curriculum/02-lesson-template.md` · **Map:** `stage0/00-stage0-unit-map.md`\n> **Arabic support:** ~25% (nearly immersion), instructions/explanations mostly English with brief\n> Arabic for hard points; learner outputs in English.\n> **Unit goal:** by the end, the learner can describe what is happening now and contrast it with what\n> usually happens — and can narrate a live scene.\n> **Grammar spine:** present continuous (am/is/are + -ing) for now · contrast with present simple (usually)\n> · **Accent Lab:** -ing endings (/ɪŋ/) + stress contrast (now vs usually) · **Mindset:** thinking in\n> English — reducing in-head translation.\n\n**Unit lessons:** L01 \"I'm …-ing\" (present continuous now) · L02 What are they doing? (scenes) ·\nL03 now vs usually (continuous vs simple) · L04 What are you wearing? (clothes + continuous) ·\nL05 Unit speaking task: describe what's happening + review.\n\n**Assets to produce:** action-verb flashcards, \"busy scene\" images (many people doing things), a\npresent-continuous clip (American model), clothes flashcards, -ing audio, a Google Form per lesson, community prompts.\n\n---\n\n## S0-U9-L01 — \"I'm …-ing\" — Present Continuous (Now)\n\n**Metadata:** Stage 0 / A1 · Unit 9 · ~30 min · recorded + live · prerequisites: Unit 8.\n\n**Objectives (can-do):**\n- The learner can say what they are doing right now using **am/is/are + -ing** (*\"I'm studying English.\"*).\n- The learner can form the -ing of common verbs (spelling: study→studying, run→running, write→writing).\n- The learner can use contractions (I'm / he's / they're + -ing).\n\n**Target language:** action verbs; *\"I'm/He's/They're + -ing (now).\"*; time markers now/at the moment.\n**Grammar:** present continuous (be + -ing) for actions in progress. **Accent Lab focus:** clean -ing\n/ɪŋ/ (recycled U5); linking *\"I'm-doing\"*.\n**Arabic support:** ~25%. **Mindset focus:** narrate your actions in English in your head (thinking in English).\n\n**Materials:** action flashcards; -ing spelling card; clip; frames slide; Google Form (make the -ing form).\n\n**Lesson flow:**\n\n| # | Phase | Content | ~Time |\n|--|--|--|--|\n| 1 | Warm-up / recall | Retrieval: give a short direction (U8) + a like with -ing (U5). | 4 min |\n| 2 | Input | Clip: \"Right now, I'm sitting at my desk. I'm drinking coffee.\"; text + brief gloss. | 6 min |\n| 3 | Notice | be + -ing for NOW; the -ing spelling rules (double letter, drop -e); contractions. | 6 min |\n| 4 | Accent Lab | Clean -ing /ɪŋ/ (sitting, drinking); link *\"I'm-sitting\"*. | 4 min |\n| 5 | Guided output | Say what you're doing now (*\"I'm ___-ing.\"*); make -ing forms from base verbs. | 6 min |\n| 6 | Free output | Live/pairs: say 3 things you're doing now (real); record one. | 3 min |\n| 7 | Feedback | Check be + -ing (don't drop the be verb) + clean -ing; queue. | — |\n| 8 | Assignment | Post a voice note: 2 things you're doing right now. -ing quiz form. | 1 min |\n\n**Differentiation:** Support — \"I'm + -ing\" only. Stretch — I'm/he's/they're + -ing across a small scene.\n**Success check:** learner forms ≥3 present-continuous sentences intelligibly. **Queue:** present continuous feeds L02–L05.\n**Coach notes:** the classic Arabic-transfer error is dropping the *be* verb (\"I studying\") — target it; also watch -ing spelling. Contrast with present simple comes in L03 (don't confuse yet).\n\n---\n\n## S0-U9-L02 — What Are They Doing? (Describing Scenes)\n\n**Metadata:** Stage 0 / A1 · Unit 9 · ~30 min · recorded + live · prerequisites: L01.\n\n**Objectives (can-do):**\n- The learner can describe what other people are doing now (*\"He's reading. They're playing.\"*).\n- The learner can ask *\"What are you/they doing?\"* and answer.\n- The learner can describe a busy scene (several actions at once).\n\n**Target language:** action verbs (3rd person continuous); *\"What is/are ___ doing?\"*\n**Grammar:** present continuous questions + he/she/they. **Accent Lab focus:** *\"What-are-they\"* linking\n(→ /wɒtə(r)ðeɪ/); clean -ing.\n**Arabic support:** ~25%. **Mindset focus:** describe scenes around you in English (a daily thinking-in-English drill).\n\n**Materials:** busy-scene images; a \"what's happening\" clip; frames slide; Google Form.\n\n**Lesson flow:**\n\n| # | Phase | Content | ~Time |\n|--|--|--|--|\n| 1 | Warm-up / recall | Retrieval: say what you're doing now (L01). | 4 min |\n| 2 | Input | Clip/scene: \"Look — he's cooking, she's talking on the phone, they're watching TV.\"; text + brief gloss. | 6 min |\n| 3 | Notice | Questions: *\"What is he doing?\" → \"He's ___-ing.\"*; multiple actions in a scene. | 6 min |\n| 4 | Accent Lab | Link *\"what-are-they-doing\"*; keep -ing clean. | 4 min |\n| 5 | Guided output | Describe a busy-scene image: 4–5 sentences (he's/she's/they're + -ing). | 6 min |\n| 6 | Free output | Live/pairs: A describes a scene, B guesses/asks *\"What is ___ doing?\"*; record. | 4 min |\n| 7 | Feedback | Check question form + he/she/they + -ing; queue. | — |\n| 8 | Assignment | Post: describe what people are doing in a photo (real or given). | — |\n\n**Differentiation:** Support — 3 sentences about a scene. Stretch — 5 sentences + a question exchange.\n**Success check:** learner describes ≥4 actions in a scene + asks a question. **Queue:** feeds L03–L05.\n**Coach notes:** great \"thinking in English\" practice — encourage narrating real surroundings. Watch dropped *be* again.\n\n---\n\n## S0-U9-L03 — now vs usually (Present Continuous vs Present Simple)\n\n**Metadata:** Stage 0 / A1 · Unit 9 · ~30 min · recorded + live · prerequisites: L02.\n\n**Objectives (can-do):**\n- The learner can contrast an action **now** (continuous) with a habit **usually** (simple).\n- The learner can choose the right form with time markers (now/at the moment vs usually/every day).\n- The learner can say *\"I usually…, but right now I'm…\"*.\n\n**Target language:** now/at the moment vs usually/every day; both tenses side by side.\n**Grammar:** present continuous vs present simple (the core A1 contrast). **Accent Lab focus:** stress the\ntime marker (*\"RIGHT now\" / \"USUALLY\"*) to signal meaning.\n**Arabic support:** ~25% (brief Arabic for the concept). **Mindset focus:** you're handling two tenses —\nreal grammatical control is forming.\n\n**Materials:** two-column visual (usually vs now); contrast clip; frames slide; Google Form (choose the tense).\n\n**Lesson flow:**\n\n| # | Phase | Content | ~Time |\n|--|--|--|--|\n| 1 | Warm-up / recall | Retrieval: a routine (present simple, U4) + a now action (L01). | 4 min |\n| 2 | Input | Clip: \"I usually drink tea, but right now I'm drinking coffee.\"; text + brief gloss. | 6 min |\n| 3 | Notice | now (continuous) vs usually (simple); time markers; explain (brief Arabic) the difference. | 7 min |\n| 4 | Accent Lab | Stress the time marker to cue meaning; clean -ing vs base verb. | 4 min |\n| 5 | Guided output | Make contrast sentences: *\"I usually ___, but right now I'm ___-ing.\"* | 6 min |\n| 6 | Free output | Live/pairs: say one habit + what you're doing differently right now; record. | 3 min |\n| 7 | Feedback | Check tense choice (the key skill) + form; queue. | — |\n| 8 | Assignment | Post: 1 \"usually\" sentence + 1 \"right now\" sentence. | — |\n\n**Differentiation:** Support — separate sentences (one of each). Stretch — combine with \"but\" in one sentence.\n**Success check:** learner correctly contrasts now vs usually in ≥2 sentences. **Queue:** the contrast recycles into U10.\n**Coach notes:** this is the highest-order grammar point in Stage 0 — keep examples vivid and personal. Don't over-explain; let the time markers do the work.\n\n---\n\n## S0-U9-L04 — What Are You Wearing? (Clothes + Continuous)\n\n**Metadata:** Stage 0 / A1 · Unit 9 · ~30 min · recorded + live · prerequisites: L03.\n\n**Objectives (can-do):**\n- The learner can name common clothes (shirt, trousers, dress, shoes, jacket, hat).\n- The learner can say what they/others are wearing now (*\"I'm wearing a blue shirt.\"*).\n- The learner can combine colours/adjectives (U7) with clothes.\n\n**Target language:** clothes vocab; *\"I'm/He's wearing ___.\"*; colours + clothes. **Grammar:** present\ncontinuous with *wear*; adjective + noun recycled (U7). **Accent Lab focus:** clear clothes words\n(shirt /ʃɜːrt/, shoes /ʃuːz/); /ʃ/ sound.\n**Arabic support:** ~25%. **Mindset focus:** you're combining old + new language freely now — that's fluency forming.\n\n**Materials:** clothes flashcards; people images; frames slide; Google Form.\n\n**Lesson flow:**\n\n| # | Phase | Content | ~Time |\n|--|--|--|--|\n| 1 | Warm-up / recall | Retrieval: now vs usually (L03) + a colour+noun (U7). | 4 min |\n| 2 | Input | Clip: \"I'm wearing a black jacket and blue jeans.\"; text + brief gloss. | 6 min |\n| 3 | Notice | Clothes vocab; *wearing + colour + item*; explain any new words. | 6 min |\n| 4 | Accent Lab | /ʃ/ in *shirt/shoes*; clear clothes words. | 4 min |\n| 5 | Guided output | Say what you're wearing (colour + item); describe a person in an image. | 6 min |\n| 6 | Free output | Live/pairs: describe what you and a classmate are wearing; record. | 4 min |\n| 7 | Feedback | Check continuous + adjective order (U7) + /ʃ/; queue. | — |\n| 8 | Assignment | Post: describe what you're wearing today (2 items with colours). | — |\n\n**Differentiation:** Support — 2 items (\"I'm wearing a shirt\"). Stretch — full outfit + describe someone else.\n**Success check:** learner describes ≥2 clothing items with colours intelligibly. **Queue:** feeds U10 review.\n**Coach notes:** nice integration of U7 (adjective+noun order) with U9 (continuous). Reinforce adjective-before-noun once more.\n\n---\n\n## S0-U9-L05 — Unit Speaking Task: Describe What's Happening (+ Review)\n\n**Metadata:** Stage 0 / A1 · Unit 9 · ~30 min · recorded + live (assessment-style) · prerequisites: L01–L04.\n\n**Objectives (can-do):**\n- The learner can narrate a scene in 6–8 sentences (what people are doing now + what they're wearing +\n  a now-vs-usually contrast).\n- The learner can ask another person what's happening / what they're doing.\n- The learner records a **\"what's happening now\"** narration (portfolio proof).\n\n**Target language:** integrated Unit-9 language. **Grammar:** present continuous + the now/usually contrast consolidated.\n**Accent Lab focus:** clean -ing + linking + /ʃ/; each learner's flagged weak sound.\n**Arabic support:** ~20% (near immersion). **Mindset focus:** you're narrating live English — you're nearly at A1 exit.\n\n**Materials:** a busy-scene image or short video; a model narration; a checklist card; recording prompt; Unit-9 quiz (Google Form).\n\n**Lesson flow:**\n\n| # | Phase | Content | ~Time |\n|--|--|--|--|\n| 1 | Warm-up / recall (retrieval-heavy) | Chain recall: present continuous, questions, now vs usually, clothes. | 5 min |\n| 2 | Input/model | Watch a model scene narration (6–7 sentences). | 4 min |\n| 3 | Notice | Break into learned frames; build the \"good scene narration\" checklist. | 4 min |\n| 4 | Accent Lab clinic | Each learner drills their weakest sound flagged in U9 (often -ing or dropped *be*). | 4 min |\n| 5 | Guided output | Rehearse the narration with the card; peer practice (pairs). | 6 min |\n| 6 | Free output (task) | Record: narrate a scene (what people are doing + wearing + one now-vs-usually contrast). | 4 min |\n| 7 | Feedback | Self + peer check vs checklist; coach group-level delayed feedback. | 2 min |\n| 8 | Assignment/queue | Post the narration; complete Unit-9 quiz; celebrate — Stage 0 assessment is next! | 1 min |\n\n**Differentiation:** Support — 6 sentences describing actions only. Stretch — full narration + contrast + a question exchange.\n**Success check (Unit-9 summary):** connected 6–8 sentence narration, intelligible, correct present continuous + basic now/usually contrast (rubric: Fluency + Accuracy + Pronunciation + Interaction at A1). **Queue:** narration recycles into Unit 10 review.\n**Coach notes:** ~10 weeks in — learners now narrate live action and juggle two tenses. This clip shows near-A1-exit fluency; log with consent. Prepare them for Unit 10 (consolidation + the summative + the graduation \"after\" sample).\n\n---\n\n## Unit 9 — end-of-unit check (formative)\n\n- **Quiz (Google Form):** -ing forms; present continuous (incl. dropped-be check); questions; now vs usually.\n- **Speaking task:** the L05 scene narration, scored on the rubric (A1 threshold; Interaction included).\n- **Feeds:** Cohort Tracker weekly activity + pronunciation notes; updates each learner's weak-sound flag.\n\n---\n\n*Next: Unit 10 — Putting It Together (consolidation + the Stage-0 summative assessment + the graduation\n\"after\" speaking sample). Completing Unit 10 finishes the entire Stage-0 Foundations curriculum.*\n"};
